window.harnessChunks["rows-9713401041bb65ab.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T17:51:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rizwan3d/StemCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StemCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rizwan3d/StemCode/tree/8e52bb02310b91337bf6eb88e163fc65acb059f8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8e52bb02310b91337bf6eb88e163fc65acb059f8\">8e52bb0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing software-engineering work by interpreting the current objective, selecting permitted inspection/edit/shell/validation actions, executing them and revising subsequent actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository state, implementation alternatives, provider uncertainty, build/test/tool failures, incomplete plans, malformed/empty provider outputs and task-specific evidence discovered during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific model/tool action and revise that choice after observing tool/provider/workspace evidence. - Decision owner: the active model-backed StemCode primary actor; delegated child actors own the same local S1 discretion for their bounded task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/execution pipeline, active profile, permission overlays, session persistence, provider adapters, plan/loop guards, workspace instructions/memory, tracked file-edit transactions and interrupt/recovery handling. - Closure path: task/current session state → provider/model decision → first-party tool execution → result persisted and returned to the conversation → same actor selects another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the first-party AgentConversationPipeline and built-in profiles are the standard runtime used by the documented CLI/Desktop/editor/headless product surfaces; no adjacent development actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining tools, permissions, sessions and deterministic guards removes the task-specific choice of what to inspect, edit, run or validate next; those mechanisms constrain or transport rather than replace that discretion. - Evidence: AgentConversationPipeline.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: particular tools may require operator approval or be denied by profile/policy, but supported operation retains autonomous task-level decision/action loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among subordinate S1 units when a primary StemCode actor coordinates several bounded child tasks against the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous editing-capable subagents could overwrite, revert or otherwise conflict with one another in the shared repository; unconstrained delegated scopes can also create cross-task churn. - Distinct S1 units: separate child ReplSessionContext executions created by agentorchestrate, each running the first-party conversation pipeline under general or explore and returning its own tool/output/edit evidence. - Inter-S1 disturbance: editing-capable child actors share the parent workspace and can therefore contend on repository mutations; StemCode&#39;s own orchestration contract distinguishes these from read-only tasks and explicitly warns coordinated agents not to revert others&#39; changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary model chooses the child task set, subagent profiles, execution strategy and optional write scopes; auto then parallelizes consecutive read-only tasks but executes editing-capable tasks in controlled sequence, while parallelreadonly rejects mutating subagents entirely. Delegated prompts transmit task/write-scope/coordination constraints to each S1. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: strategy and write-scope choices alter when child S1s run and what workspace region they are instructed to touch; each child handoff/tool/edit result returns to the parent actor, which can integrate the evidence into subsequent operation or another coordinated handoff. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is explicitly conditioned on the concrete interference class of shared-workspace mutations. Read-only work is allowed to overlap; editing-capable work is deliberately serialized or rejected from the parallel mode, and bounded write scopes are passed to mutating children. This is targeted disturbance attenuation, not sequencing merely because tasks exist. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which bounded child activities should coexist, which agent profile should perform each, what coordination strategy to use and what write boundaries to assign before those S1s operate. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed StemCode actor owns the task-specific coordination discretion; deterministic orchestration code enforces the nonparallel mutation safety rule and concurrency limit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentOrchestrateTool; child-session construction; MaxParallelReadOnlyTasks; editing-capable classification; strategy validation; semaphore; sequential execution of mutating requests; delegated coordination/write-scope prompt; returned aggregate result. - Closure path: primary actor identifies independent/dependent side work → calls agentorchestrate with tasks/strategy/scopes → runtime schedules read-only parallelism and mutation serialization → child S1s receive coordination constraints and operate → their results/edit evidence return to the primary actor → later primary/child behaviour can be revised. - Boundary reachability: agentorchestrate is in the standard build profile and in supported inspection-oriented primary profiles where applicable; child general/explore profiles and the execution policy are shipped first-party runtime behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the primary actor is removed, deterministic code can still serialize a pre-existing request, but no task-specific actor remains to decide the coordinated task set, child profiles, strategy or write scopes. The runtime owns enforcement; the primary agent owns the bounded coordination choice. - Evidence: AgentOrchestrateTool.cs; AgentDelegationSupport.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this S2 is bounded to one orchestration handoff and the shared-workspace interference it regulates; it does not imply persistent organization-wide current control.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S3 whole-current control loop was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task decomposition, active-plan tracking, permission enforcement, orchestration batch scheduling and token/budget limits exist, but no distinct whole-system current-operation variety is shown being regulated through S3 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live plan/update-plan machinery, bounded subagent orchestration, permission profiles, budget controls, session state, interrupts and deterministic concurrency limits. - Closure path: absent at S3 level; no first-party persistent whole-system current view → shared-resource/commitment/priority intervention → returned change to an independently evolving operational population was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the primary model does allocate bounded delegated work, but that is parent-task decomposition inside one awaited orchestration call. The reviewed path does not expose the whole-system current view and current-control authority required by the Profile. - Evidence: AgentOrchestrateTool.cs; BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; AgentConversationPipeline.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future/background-task machinery could change this conclusion if it supplied a population view plus substantive intervention rights; that path is not established in the frozen revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide an audit-specific, read-only review path over a proposed change set that is separate from the ordinary implementation actor and can challenge its claims with findings about bugs, regressions, unsafe assumptions, edge cases and missing tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that the normal implementation/reporting path may miss defects or weak tests in a PR/change set. - Claim being audited: that a proposed repository change is correct/safe enough with no material unreported regressions or testing gaps. - Ordinary reporting path: the ordinary coding actor edits/runs validation and reports completion through its primary session/tool loop; routine build/test results remain part of that production path. - Complementary access path: shipped GitHub review automation computes the independent base/head diff, invokes a separate read-only pr-reviewer profile against that artifact and publishes findings as a PR review/comment, with an uploaded review artifact. - Independence boundary: the reviewer is a separately invoked StemCode session/profile with read-only/safe-inspection permissions and receives the PR diff rather than inheriting the implementation actor&#39;s conversational claims. However the committed workflow&#39;s PR trigger is commented out and the path does not autonomously drive corrective execution back into the ordinary coding actor. - Who acts on findings: the hosting-platform/user/developer workflow consuming the posted PR findings; first-party StemCode does not in this frozen standard setup automatically convert those findings into a corrective implementation turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party tooling exposes the audit-specific independent judgment and outbound findings path, but the autonomous corrective authority/return closure must still be composed by the adopter. - Decision owner: constructor state. StemCode supplies the independent model-backed reviewer and findings publication primitive; deployment wiring and downstream corrective authority remain outside the closed autonomous product loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pr-reviewer read-only profile; base/head diff construction; review script; GitHub workflow permissions/artifact upload; PR comment/review publication. - Closure path: first-party path reaches PR findings publication but stops before autonomous corrective control; an adopter must compose the findings-consumer/repair return path for full closure. - Boundary reachability: the audit path is explicitly shipped and documented as StemCode CI review automation with first-party scripts/profile/workflow, so the S3-specific constructor is reachable without borrowing a third-party reviewer. The missing autonomous corrective return is why the state is C, not A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment itself is made by a separate model-backed reviewer, not by a deterministic parser. The incomplete part is organizational closure from findings back into corrective operation, so the assessment does not publish A. - Evidence: .github/stemcode-github-review.sh; .github/workflows/stemcode-review.yml; .stemcode/agents/pr-reviewer.md; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository co-location alone is not used as evidence. The positive state is intentionally only the shipped audit-specific constructor path; ordinary review profile use inside the same production conversation and routine tests are not credited as S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop over StemCode&#39;s own current capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web search, codebase intelligence, repository/team memory and optional lessons expose useful information for task execution and reuse, but they do not establish S4 at the assessed organizational recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: websearch, headless browser, codebase/LSP tools, repo memory, lesson memory, automatic tool-failure observation, skills and configurable profiles/providers. - Closure path: no external/future distinction → adaptation-option development → return into present organizational capability/S3 loop was established. Lesson retrieval returns prior local mistakes/fixes to future prompts but is not an external-and-prospective adaptation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can use web/repository evidence for the current task and can save/retrieve lessons, but that is current-task sensing/learning unless tied to future-oriented organizational adaptation of capability. - Evidence: BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; StemCode/Application/Tools/LessonMemoryTool.cs; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future support for agent-owned capability/profile/tool adaptation driven by environmental/future modeling could establish S4; current reusable memory alone does not.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, profiles, workspace instructions, budgets, approval prompts and operator configuration constrain ordinary operation, but no S5-level identity/policy issue is adjudicated through a runtime ultimate-authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5; developers/operators author configuration and ordinary approval choices outside any qualifying first-party S5 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in profile permission overlays; allow/deny/ask rules; workspace files/instructions; provider/model configuration; budget controls; operator prompts and runtime enforcement. - Closure path: absent at S5 level; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing subsequent operation path was found in the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model is constrained by configured policy but does not own the ultimate right to redefine StemCode&#39;s identity or top-level policy; static configuration and ordinary tool approval are specifically insufficient for S5. - Evidence: BuiltInAgentProfiles.cs; README.md; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository maintainer governance is adjacent to the product boundary and is not imported as runtime S5 merely because it determines future releases.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/52c1740f0432c2d27847af8501de8f2772f55a86/assessments/st/stemcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 52c1740f0432c2d27847af8501de8f2772f55a86\">52c1740 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T19:15:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/witt3rd/oh-my-hermes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Oh My Hermes</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/witt3rd/oh-my-hermes/tree/3b06e33ae67b202b342989b484f08143e65e250d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3b06e33ae67b202b342989b484f08143e65e250d\">3b06e33 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify individual software-plan tasks using model-guided agents and actual project tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain implementation paths, changing repository files, build/test failures, unexpected tool results and acceptance feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what code/commands to use, revise an implementation after verifier feedback and produce task artifacts. - Decision owner: Hermes parent/executor model agents invoked by first-party OMH ralph/autopilot skill modes; external Hermes supplies the model runtime, not the organizational task choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegatetask, OMH role hooks, work-state persistence, planned acceptance criteria, evidence command execution and progress logs. - Closure path: a plan task is delegated to an executor; source changes and tool feedback are returned; the separate verdict can cause targeted rework, task completion or a blocked state, changing subsequent operating action. - Boundary reachability: README documents skill and optional plugin installation; omh-ralph/SKILL.md explicitly invokes a provider-backed delegated executor within that supported installed mode, while plugin prellmcall injects packaged executor role context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects implementation actions under task/role constraints; plugin code only stores and enforces the workflow. - Evidence: Ralph Steps 3–6, role injection hook, delegation boundary. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for supported skill mode, not actual task quality. - Caveats: requires a separately installed Hermes runtime/model and user/caller re-invocation; no live provider-backed workflow was executed for this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate collisions among parallel task workers and between two live OMH sessions targeting the same plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent task agents writing overlapping files and separate workflow sessions racing on the same plan/state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which tasks can safely run in parallel and whether an invocation may proceed with a particular workflow instance. - Decision owner: skill-following Hermes coordinator chooses independent task groups and responds to lock-denied feedback; deterministic OMH state tool implements the keyed lock and refuses conflicting holders. The full function requires explicit installed skill/plugin composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-instance advisory locks in omhstate, key/holder records, Ralph and Autopilot acquisition/release discipline, dependency/file-footprint checks and cancellation. - Closure path: before work, the skill asks the state tool to lock a project plan; a conflicting holder causes wait/cancel/other-plan feedback rather than two writers proceeding on the same plan. Within a run, the coordinator selects only independent tasks for parallel delegation and feeds results into later task choice. - Boundary reachability: both installed omh-ralph and omh-autopilot explicitly call shipped omhstate(action=&quot;lock&quot;), and the registered statetool.py dispatches to lock acquire/release/check; no hypothetical third-party coordination plugin is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C records the explicitly composed agent decision plus state-based contention mechanism; fixed mutex implementation alone is not promoted to an autonomous S2 decision owner. - Distinct S1 units: parallel executor child agents within Ralph; competing skill-driven workflow sessions for one plan form the second, narrower interference case. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-file overlap or simultaneous updates to the same .omh task/plan state. - Attenuating coordination relation: dependence and file-footprint screening before parallel delegates, plus keyed plan/session lock and conflict response. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a denied lock pauses/reroutes a would-be session; only noninterfering workers receive parallel tasks, and their reports affect the coordinator&#39;s next eligible-task selection. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path addresses explicit competing writer/session interference, not mere message delivery or parent-to-child sequencing; the state lock is connected to the skill&#39;s alternate action on contention. - Evidence: Ralph lock and parallel selection, Autopilot contention response, state tool, state engine. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: advisory rather than universally enforced across independent Hermes instances; plugin-free skills and arbitrary independent writers do not inherit this guarantee. Coordination within unrelated projects is not assessed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current whole-workflow task commitments, retries, completion and failure handling across delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck plan dependencies, incomplete acceptance criteria, repeated identical errors, unfinished tasks, conflicting runtime-phase progress and tool/test failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose eligible tasks, batch or defer work, initiate corrective retries, mark tasks passed or blocked, and determine current phase progression under shared constraints. - Decision owner: skill-following Hermes coordinating agent inside the supported Ralph/Autopilot mode; first-party state machinery tracks and enforces its choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: omhstate plan/task records, prerequisite gate, dependency graph, max-iterations and three-strike limits, omhgatherevidence, verification verdicts and cancellation. - Closure path: coordinator reads whole-plan/task status, assigns eligible work, receives executor and evidence-based verifier outcomes, writes changed pass/block/retry status, and the next invocation uses this state to alter commitments and later dispatches. - Boundary reachability: omh-ralph is a shipped installable skill with explicit Steps 1–8 and whole-plan state transitions; omh-autopilot repeatedly checks Ralph state and advances/presents blocked current work. The actor is reachable via the installed Hermes tool boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing what whole-plan work is committed, retried or stopped depends on the coordinator&#39;s context-sensitive judgment, not solely on deterministic state-file writes or raw task delegation. - Whole-system current view: active phase, plan/task queue, dependency status, retries, evidence verdicts, error fingerprints, cancellation/lock state and current invocation. - Current-control decision scope: work allocation and acceptance across all current tasks in a project workflow, not a child agent&#39;s local tool choice or future identity decision. - Evidence: Ralph orchestration and recovery, Autopilot phase controller, state engine. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user/caller still launches each separate invocation. The system does not autonomously continue if nobody re-invokes it; current decisions within a reached invocation are the claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently distinct nonroutine complementary audit of ordinary execution claims established in the supported OMH work boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an executor or coordinator can misreport success, and normal tests may miss defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier and reviewer roles issue PASS/FAIL or REQUESTCHANGES in mandatory delivery/QA phases, but no separately controlled sporadic audit decision is established beyond that ordinary path. - Decision owner: routine Hermes verifier/reviewer and same-workflow coordinator own acceptance, not a separately evidenced S3 audit function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only verifier prompt, fresh command output, QA and parallel validation reviewers, research citation checks and plan criticism. - Closure path: failed verifier/reviewer judgments lead to executor retry or a blocked state within the normal production pipeline; a distinct complementary audit escalation/alternative observation path is not shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model role and real fresh test output strengthen routine quality control but do not by themselves provide independent sporadic access outside the standard success-report path. - Evidence: Ralph Step 5 verification, Autopilot QA/validation, verifier role, evidence tool. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an external independent auditor could inspect project artifacts, but that would be adjacent to rather than demonstrated inside this product mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environmental scanning and strategic capability adaptation of OMH itself established in the assessed project workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external tool/model capabilities, project environment and future requirements might require adaptation beyond the currently requested work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the shipped research skill can synthesize outside facts for a user&#39;s research or planning task, but no agent-owned mechanism determines OMH&#39;s future capability policy and returns such options into its own present operational configuration. - Decision owner: project users define research/feature goals; current-task planners execute them. OMH maintainers&#39; future roadmap is adjacent development, not in-boundary S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: omh-deep-research, omh-ralplan, persistent plans/specs, task learnings forwarded to later executors and roadmap documents. - Closure path: a topic-specific report or implementation plan can inform the next user task, but there is no established outside-and-then system self-adaptation judgment with a durable capability change owned by the runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external research as an S1 deliverable, feedback on the present coding task and stored learnings are not automatically a future-oriented S4 conversation with present capability. - Evidence: deep research scope, Ralplan purpose, Autopilot phases. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: specialized user-authored strategic workflows could be composed on top of OMH; generic possibility is not a shipped S4 ownership path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported identity- or ultimate-policy dispute/decision path established for a deployed OMH workflow as distinct from normal task authorization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential conflicts about what the multi-agent workflow ultimately exists to do and who may redefine its identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users confirm requirements or approve risky plans, but no shipped ultimate identity-policy judgment is routed to a legitimate S5 owner and returned as governing operating policy. - Decision owner: normal local user for specifications/cancellation and upstream project maintainers for product evolution; neither has a documented in-boundary S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: interview confirmation, Ralplan consensus, triage sign-off, command allowlists, plugin configuration and cancellation. - Closure path: these mechanisms can govern an ordinary task transition; no distinct charter/purpose dispute → legitimate policy authority → binding decision → subsequent identity-governed operation path was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static skill purpose, safety allowlist and user sign-off cannot by themselves be equated with ultimate-policy ownership. - Evidence: deep interview user-confirmed scope, triage sign-off, config. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: higher-recursion human governance may exist outside this one local workflow; it is not borrowed into this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96d75706f77a718a6173501a19ce98b9f76bceb8/assessments/oh/oh-my-hermes.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96d75706f77a718a6173501a19ce98b9f76bceb8\">96d7570 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T04:23:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/get-vix/vix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/get-vix/vix/tree/e68a25c7df685335f5a202844988d492fe3c1b16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e68a25c7df685335f5a202844988d492fe3c1b16\">e68a25c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify software to satisfy a coding objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/test failures, context variation, provider responses and bounded delegated investigations/implementation work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive code/tool actions, interpret evidence and decide how to satisfy the engineering objective. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Vix agent or a model-backed workflow/subagent step owning its bounded operational outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon tool dispatch, sandbox/permission checks, workflow engine, session state, provider adapters, timeouts and budgets. - Closure path: objective → agent selects coding/tool action → runtime executes and returns evidence → agent revises work until completion/blocking. - Boundary reachability: the standard interactive agent, workflow agent steps and scheduled headless runs all invoke the shipped first-party model/tool runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic machinery carries/enforces actions; the model chooses the substantive engineering response. - Evidence: README.md; internal/config/defaults/agents/general.md; internal/daemon/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tools and workflows constrain the action surface without owning the coding judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material standard-distribution path is established that specifically attenuates an evidenced interference/oscillation between distinct operational S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance is placed under a first-party coordination loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent, background task IDs, taskoutput, workflow edges/forks, per-step budgets and separate threads transport/decompose work but do not themselves regulate a concrete peer-operational collision. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models can delegate or run parallel children, but delegation/parallelism alone does not establish coordination; no standard collision/oscillation response with feedback into both/affected S1 units was found. - Distinct S1 units: independent spawned agents or workflow/job agent steps can own bounded outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous write-capable units could conflict in shared project state, but the reviewed standard path does not document a corresponding S2-specific attenuation relation. - Attenuating coordination relation: none established beyond generic sequencing/routing/thread isolation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: no S2-specific collision result/negotiation path established. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not; the inspected primitives remain generic delegation/workflow transport for this purpose. - Evidence: internal/daemon/subagent.go; internal/daemon/toolschemas.go; internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a custom workflow can serialize work, but static workflow ordering is not sufficient S2 evidence under the active Profile.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous or constructor S3 current-control loop is established at the declared standard boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no whole-system current operational disturbance is placed under a first-party supervisory decision right. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow budgets/conditions, job scheduler state, timeout/auto-disable, task lists and user plan approval enforce or expose selected constraints but do not supply the required whole-system current-control discretion. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary agents own local task execution; deterministic workflow/scheduler machinery owns no discretionary whole-system S3 judgment, and the Plan approval gate occurs before execution rather than regulating a live operational whole. - Whole-system current view: job/thread/workflow status surfaces exist, but no standard autonomous owner is wired to use them as a whole-system current-control view. - Current-control decision scope: no qualifying standard owner/path established over current resource/commitment/priorities on behalf of the whole. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json; internal/daemon/jobs/scheduler.go; internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users can approve plans and manage jobs; those local/configuration interventions are not promoted to S3 without the Profile&#39;s whole-system current-control closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit an operational agent&#39;s completion claim against current project state and return gaps into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing requirements, unverifiable implementation or defects that ordinary worker self-report can miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make an independent pass/fail audit judgment from direct project evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed verify/reviewer agent running in a distinct workflow step/context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal workflow routing, structured JSON verdict, read/bash/code-intelligence tool access and deterministic pass/fail branch. - Closure path: pursue agent signals complete → separate verify agent independently inspects current state → verifier returns pass/fail plus feedback → fail routes feedback into a new pursue iteration; pass allows wrap-up. - Boundary reachability: Goal is a bundled standard workflow and its verifier/pursue routing is shipped in the default workflow configuration; reviewer agent/prompt is bundled first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workflow code routes the result, but the separate model-backed auditor makes the evidence-based judgment. - Claim being audited: the operational worker&#39;s claim that the requested goal is complete. - Ordinary reporting path: pursue&#39;s completion signal and ordinary implementation/tool results. - Complementary access path: a separate verifier/reviewer can read current repository state, run checks and form its own evidence-based verdict rather than trusting the worker transcript. - Independence boundary: distinct workflow agent step/reviewer role with its own run context and evidence-gathering tools; the Goal verify prompt is explicitly an independent audit. - Who acts on findings: deterministic workflow routing returns rejected-audit feedback to the operational pursue agent, which must address the gaps before signalling completion again. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/config/workflow.json; internal/config/defaults/prompts/goal/verify.md; internal/config/defaults/agents/reviewer.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 is credited to the independent evidence path and corrective return, not merely to the name &quot;reviewer&quot; or to ordinary self-tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: construct recurring external/future sensing workflows whose model-backed reaction can develop and apply adaptation to the current project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future time-based needs and changing external conditions such as new pull requests, dependency/security changes, remote state or other polled events. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the composed scheduled agent step can interpret newly sensed external evidence and choose an adaptive project response; the standard distribution exposes the sensing, gating, model-run and unattended-write path, while the concrete adaptation objective/authority is supplied by the composed job/workflow. - Decision owner: constructor state — Vix supplies the function-specific first-party primitives and an autonomous general-agent runtime, but the developer/operator/creating agent must compose the concrete external distinction, prospective objective and return policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon cron/at scheduler, prompt resolution, bash polling, executeif gating, workflow agent steps, auto-write permissions, timeout/budget controls and persisted job state. - Closure path: composed external/time trigger → first-party polling/sensing step → conditional model-backed agent interprets the changed environment and develops a response → unattended workflow may write/act in project cwd → subsequent project capability/operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the jobs skill and scheduler are shipped first-party; jobs run through the daemon as isolated headless Vix threads and can invoke named/inline workflows using the standard general agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the eventual reacting agent can be autonomous, but the standard distribution does not pre-close one concrete external-and-prospective adaptation conversation; composition supplies the environmental model/objective/authority, so the published base state is C. - Evidence: internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md; internal/daemon/jobs/scheduler.go; internal/daemon/jobrunner.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a reminder or event reaction alone is not S4; C rests on the first-party watcher/workflow construction path being specifically capable of sensing external/future distinctions, invoking an autonomous option-developing agent and returning action into project capability. - External distinction: the polling recipe explicitly senses changes outside the current coding run, such as newly opened pull requests or analogous remote/project-environment events. - Future / prospective distinction: cron/at triggers and persisted jobs defer sensing and model judgment to later environmental states rather than only reacting inside the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: the conditionally invoked general agent can interpret the new external evidence and develop a project response, such as a risk treatment or code/configuration change, under the composed workflow objective. - Path back into current capability / S3: the scheduled agent runs in the target project cwd with workflow/tool authority (including unattended writes when enabled), so a composed adaptation decision can change current project capability/operation; Vix supplies this return path while the concrete S4 loop remains constructor-composed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level conflict/proposal is routed through authoritative S5 closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sandbox, deny lists, workflow denytools, permission/confirmation surfaces, prompts and configuration constrain local operation without becoming identity authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents operate within developer/operator-authored policy and cannot authoritatively redefine Vix&#39;s identity or ultimate governing principles. - Evidence: README.md; internal/config/defaults/skills/jobs/SKILL.md; internal/daemon/sandbox.go. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human confirmation and permissions are operational safety decisions, not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ee4c77759afcb8c999e46092bd300973e782fced/assessments/vix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ee4c77759afcb8c999e46092bd300973e782fced\">ee4c777 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/ClawGUI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ClawGUI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/ClawGUI/tree/d990d3e3f2390ced708771d9681d3c4f9a6cbef5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d990d3e3f2390ced708771d9681d3c4f9a6cbef5\">d990d3e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: execute user-requested GUI/device outcomes through autonomous perception, planning/tool orchestration and screen actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing UI state, device/application differences, task instructions, failed actions and environmental interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven agent actors choose operational plans/actions and adapt to observed device state. - Decision owner: autonomous brain/GUI-agent actor in the first-party deployment path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: device adapters, sessions, memory, chat surfaces and skill retrieval/injection. - Closure path: actions change device state, subsequent screen observations return to the agent and drive later actions until completion/failure. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - The two-agent brain/phone architecture separates responsibilities and tool execution, but the pinned evidence does not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation plus a mutual-adjustment loop that attenuates it. Orchestration/delegation alone is not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The brain orchestrates a task and the runtime manages sessions/device execution, but no distinct first-party actor is evidenced with current whole-organization visibility plus discretionary authority over shared resources, priorities or commitments across multiple operational S1s. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A. - Claim being audited: whether the operating PhoneAgent actually achieved the user&#39;s GUI task and, when not, where observable execution first departed from success. - Ordinary reporting path: executor rollout/actions and its normal task outcome. - Complementary access path: an explicitly independent verifier receives only sealed external evidence — screenshots, action log, accessibility/DOM data, final state and optional prior assertions — and never sees the executor&#39;s reasoning, plans or skill package. - Independence boundary: the verifier is instructed to judge only observable evidence and cannot modify the executor&#39;s playbook itself. - Decisive judgment / owner: the verifier model independently determines task success, failure type/step, root cause and behavioral corrections. - Supporting mechanisms: strict JSON schema, evidence packaging and restricted skill-file tools. - Closure path: failed verification feeds a separate skillrevise step that is required to change the skill package; when a revision is produced, PhoneAgent immediately retries using the changed capability. - Why this is S3: the path provides materially different access to operational reality, an independent judgment boundary and explicit corrective return rather than routine executor self-checking. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - The evolve loop changes future procedural capability, but its trigger and evidence are failures from the system&#39;s own current GUI-task execution. It does not establish an external-and-prospective model of changing users, threats, tools or future conditions that develops adaptation options in a two-way S3/S4 conversation. - Self-improvement alone is therefore not promoted to S4. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - User requests, model/configuration choices, skill modes and restricted edit surfaces constrain operation, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is evidenced at this recursion. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/clawgui.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T19:41:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/senweaver/SenWeaverCoding\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SenWeaverCoding</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/senweaver/SenWeaverCoding/tree/8ba81d595ba154f300428dd554d2065b9a268432\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8ba81d595ba154f300428dd554d2065b9a268432\">8ba81d5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-mediated software development from user request through file/tool operations, testing/observation and subsequent repair or response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous coding tasks, unexpected tool outputs, compiler/test failures, file contents and changed instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tools or code actions based on previous responses and produce/revise a user-facing task result. - Decision owner: the active configured model in the first-party agent/unified loop and, when selected, agentic delegated role workers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: IDE/headless CLI, provider adapters, tool registry, unified loop policy, file/shell/git tools, conversation messages and subagent runtime. - Closure path: human goal → model chooses actions → first-party tool effects → observed results in model context → next model action, repair, response or bounded stop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses tool sequence and changes its actions on feedback; the Rust executor supplies capabilities and limits rather than deterministically prescribing all task decisions. - Evidence: agent entry, unified loop and delegated agentic worker in pinned source links above. - Basis: reachable model-run source path with real tool effects and returned observations. - Confidence: medium-high for supported configuration. - Caveats: outcome quality, model credentials and live execution are not validated by this static source review. - Boundary reachability: src/agent/agent.rs invokes UnifiedLoop::run, and delegateparallel is a first-party registered tool callable from the model/tool loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate cross-worker file/edit contention and task-order conflicts during concurrent delegated coding. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agentic subagents modifying shared files, simultaneous Git updates, dirty parent workspace, dependency inversions and excessive parallelism. - Decisive decision or feedback right: isolate workers in Git worktrees, serialize base-workspace merges, preserve branches when parent is dirty, and limit/sequence DAG execution. - Decision owner: constructor-specified scheduler, file/region lock manager, worktree and merge guards, not an independently policy-setting coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task dependency graph, subagent limiter, basemergelock, named worktree creation, commit/merge and salvage checks, region and buffer locks. - Closure path: concurrent worker requests → graph/limit checks and separate workspaces → bounded parallel execution → serialized guarded merge or salvaged output → merged status/failure metadata available to parent and subsequent workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S2 handles a concrete interference relation between distinct active S1 workers; guards and conditional concurrency are source-defined rules rather than model-owned autonomous negotiation. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated agentic model workers with their own task prompt, provider invocation, allowed tools and working directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits or merge attempts contaminate the same codebase and can invalidate siblings&#39; results. - Attenuating coordination relation: each worker gets an isolated named worktree where available; base-workspace merge is lock-serialized, dirty parents defer merging, and scheduler capacity/dependencies govern dispatch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: merged output exposes per-task successes/failures, degraded fallback and merge notes; failed/dirty merges preserve branches and report conditions to the parent, altering next task execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: worktree isolation and the per-base exclusive merge lock directly suppress simultaneous file-state interference between multiple independently coding S1s. - Evidence: src/tools/delegate/parallel.rs worktree/merge/return path, src/agent/coordination.rs, multi-agent runtime and merge module (links above). - Basis: runtime code with concrete conflict attenuation, not just agent messaging or a parallelism claim. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: when worktree creation fails workers can use a shared workspace under file locks; implementation does not prove freedom from every possible merge conflict. - Boundary reachability: model-accessible delegateparallel executes the scheduler/worktree code and returns merge/failure metadata to the calling model.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model parent makes present whole-task control decisions using tool results, delegated execution outcomes and failures to choose next steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task feasibility, partial subagent failures, unavailable capabilities/providers, ambiguous merged results, tool timeouts and integration failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decompose or pursue a task directly, select/delegate work and merge style, inspect aggregate success/degraded status, retry or change approach before final user response. - Decision owner: top-level model agent operating the unified loop and issuing delegateparallel tool calls; deterministic scheduling/merging supports its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conversation and tool state, task graph, registry/blackboard, MergedOutput failures, optional LLM merge, bounded tool limits and cancellation. - Closure path: parent user goal → top-level model selects task execution/delegation → scheduler runs subordinate S1 agents → merged outputs/failed-task metadata return as a tool result → parent model re-evaluates current work, acts or ends. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the top-level model&#39;s future tool selections depend on feedback about the task as a whole, whereas graph dispatch/health supervision alone are deterministic support. - Whole-system current view: aggregate subtask success/degradation, merge results/failures, tool observations and current conversation/workspace state visible to the parent for the assigned coding task. - Current-control decision scope: whether to invoke subagents, which tasks/dependencies/merge mode to request, and whether the present task needs more actions or is ready for a response. - Evidence: src/agent/agent.rs, src/agent/loop/unified.rs, src/tools/delegate/parallel.rs lines covering task submission/result serialization, src/coordinator/delegation.rs. - Basis: model ownership of current task orchestration with closed worker-result return route. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is task-scoped S3, not ownership of external product release, organization portfolio, user permissions or a guaranteed multi-agent deployment. - Boundary reachability: the registered delegation tool returns serialized merged/failure metadata to the same model-aware unified loop that determines subsequent tool calls.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: internal judges and verification provide task quality signals, but no independently bounded challenger of the parent S3 decision is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect answers, weak tool outcomes, next-turn user corrections and possible integration faults. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately challenge an S3 completion claim using independently held complementary evidence and route a finding into its corrective authority. - Decision owner: optional provider-backed next-state reward judge, LLM candidate merger and verification pipeline operate under the same runtime/configuration; independent S3 decision owner is not demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution reward queue, previous/next conversation state, self-reflection lessons, verification pipeline and optional LLM result synthesis. - Closure path: local task result → internal judge/reflection/reward or optional merge → assistant learning/report; no demonstrated blind independent S3-audit scope and binding corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: using a second model call as a score/judge is not sufficient evidence of complementary governance independence; this is internal evaluation of the same task or output. - Evidence: src/evolution/judge.rs, reflection worker, src/coordinator/delegation.rs and src/agent/verification/pipeline.rs. - Basis: independence-and-return-path test; ordinary self-check and reward are not S3. - Confidence: medium-high for scoped absence. - Caveats: an external reviewer could be installed by a user, but that independent institution is not part of the reviewed first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: optional self-reflection/evolution learns from historical turns, but does not demonstrate ongoing prospective external-environment sensing with future capability choices for the system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated assistant mistakes, weak tool outcomes, user corrections and deteriorating local work patterns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: scan the outside future environment, select strategic adaptations beyond the present task, and return actionable options into current control. - Decision owner: internal evolution/reflection agents and constructor writeback controls for local lessons; no separately evidenced prospective S4 decision-maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: next-state reward judge, reflection worker, lesson writeback, history/distiller and optional web-search tool for ordinary user tasks. - Closure path: past answer and next-turn outcome → reward/lesson extraction → locally stored guidance; no traced external future signals → strategic option → S3 adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven past-performance reflection is real but does not on its own satisfy outward-looking future intelligence; ad hoc browser/search tools are not proof of a continuing S4 mandate. - Evidence: src/evolution/judge.rs, src/evolution/reflection/reflector.rs, src/evolution/distiller.rs, web-search registration in src/agent/agent.rs. - Basis: external-future versus retrospective internal-learning distinction. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specific user could direct a research task, but that is S1 work within the supplied goal and not systematic own-system S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: configuration and agent operating modes specify limits but do not grant authority to redefine legitimate overall system purpose or ultimate policy conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider permissions, credentials, tool restrictions, risk/approval constraints, model choices and user intent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the system&#39;s binding identity and top-level normative priorities when objectives conflict, with legitimate authority over the other functions. - Decision owner: external user, repository maintainers and configured permissions/providers set the top-level purpose and rules; the runtime supplies enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user settings, tool allowlists, configured model/provider, safety boundaries, agent mode and runtime guardrails. - Closure path: external user policy/request → configured restrictions → model executes bounded task or stops; no first-party final constitutional policy adjudication. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model discretion over coding steps and optional lesson generation is not independent legitimate authority to revise the harness&#39;s identity or user mandate. - Evidence: README modes, src/agent/agent.rs configuration/tool routing, delegate tool allowed-tool/max-iteration controls. - Basis: ultimate policy-right ownership boundary. - Confidence: high for scoped absence. - Caveats: S5 may exist in a larger human-led enterprise, but is not inherited into this runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5c66295f4ba0236a889ad9dee9426cf9efdfef31/assessments/se/senweavercoding.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5c66295f4ba0236a889ad9dee9426cf9efdfef31\">5c66295 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T23:02:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Meta-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent/tree/28e18519eba79e0e97217989390704167c43a5c8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 28e18519eba79e0e97217989390704167c43a5c8\">28e1851 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform model-led task-solving harness execution and generate actionable revised harness programs under the optimizer&#39;s run objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous benchmark tasks, failures, noisy reward/trace outcomes and implementation defects in candidate programs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: task-facing program harness calls the configured model and chooses task response/actions; model-backed proposer diagnoses examples and writes new program/prompt/tool changes. - Decision owner: model-backed target harness in task cells, and model-backed proposer for operative candidate generation, with the external model provider furnishing computation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: benchmark adapters, task runner, proposer invocation, staging directories, validation/smoke checks and run budget. - Closure path: benchmark task → candidate model/harness actions → result/trace/reward → proposed code changes → next candidate evaluation. - Boundary reachability: CLI loop invokes task-runner and invokeproposer in its shipped epoch.py code path, with explicit backend/benchmark choices. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents choose meaningful answers and candidate edits; Python scripts validate and schedule their choices. - Evidence: README.md (harness API/CLI), loop/epoch.py, loop/proposer.py, taskrunner/program.py. - Basis: explicit/structural. - Confidence: high for wired source path, no success benchmark independently rerun. - Caveats: user supplies benchmark/model credentials; local task harnesses may operate under different target adapters.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated function-specific attenuation of disturbance among distinct simultaneously acting S1 operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple candidate harnesses can be evaluated and selected, but their score comparison is an adaptation/selection process, not inter-S1 conflict/oscillation regulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: multi-candidate workflow persists separate results and picks the higher reward; no identified live interference/negotiation right between independent operating cells. - Decision owner: deterministic selection policy and configured proposer for candidate exploration, not a team coordination authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated candidate directories, fan-out evaluation, structured results and reward sorting. - Closure path: candidate scores feed selection of future harness, not an S2-specific interference signal returned to current colliding S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel experiments and comparison do not by themselves supply VSM coordination at the declared recursion. - Evidence: loop/epoch.py (runoneepochmulti, holdout fanout, winner selection), core/experience.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded experiment pipeline. - Caveats: downstream target harnesses could have their own multi-agent coordination, not inherited by the meta-optimizer.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate present performance across task-sample cells and control which validated harness configuration becomes the accepted whole-run champion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local candidate changes may overfit seen failures, fail smoke tests or regress aggregate task accuracy/cost. - Whole-system current view: LoopState.history, baseline/epoch score summaries and optional held-out aggregate metrics summarize current results across candidate/task evaluations. - Current-control decision scope: reject structurally invalid candidates, compare aggregate search/holdout performance and choose the next working parent/best-so-far harness under the run&#39;s configured acceptance criterion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: code computes reward, evaluates all eligible siblings and updates bestrate/parent selection; with --accept-on-holdout, holdout score can gate promotion. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party loop carrying a developer/operator-defined scoring and acceptance policy; no autonomous agent owns the decisive global acceptance criterion in the standard run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validateconfig, smokecandidate, score aggregation, archived experience and best-rate history. - Closure path: task-cell outcomes → aggregate score/current candidate comparison → selected best parent → next proposer context and subsequent candidate run. - Boundary reachability: epoch.py calls selectparentcandidatename and updates the champion in the shipped CLI, including optional but first-party holdout acceptance mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model proposer writes possible adaptations; deterministic acceptance and experiment configuration choose the installed champion based on numeric criterion, so ownership remains constructor/control machinery rather than agent discretionary S3. - Evidence: loop/epoch.py (finalizeepoch, runoneepochmulti, winneridx), loop/state.py, loop/validate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; selection is run-level rather than continuous production governance. - Caveats: holdout may fail and source intentionally falls back to search reward; this weakens external-validity protection, not the existence of the selection path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate independent spot inspection of actual operations with corrective feedback into S3 beyond the ordinary score/validation path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: flawed or misleading candidate results are caught by validation, smoke tests and scoring, which form normal run acceptance rather than a separate audit function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: held-out evaluation may use unseen tasks and a judge/label distinct from the proposer, but it is the configured ordinary acceptance metric rather than an independent complementary inspection channel. - Decision owner: benchmark/scoring adapters and fixed run policy; no separately independent audit judgment owner with authority to challenge S3 reports. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proposer-readable trace restrictions, holdout aggregate isolation, config validation, smoke runs and score records. - Closure path: held-out score enters the ordinary best-candidate selector; no audit finding reaches a distinct supervisory corrective path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independence of a validation split from proposer prompts does not by itself establish organizational complementary audit of current operations. - Evidence: loop/epoch.py (stripholdoutfrommeta, runevaluation, finalizeepoch), taskrunner/judgerunner.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: benchmark quality, blind independence and label reliability are external and not independently verified here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense failures in the external task/benchmark environment, formulate prospective changes to the harness decision procedure and feed viable options back to the active capability configuration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes and differences in task demand, failure patterns, judge feedback, tool/reasoning deficits and overfitting risk across unseen tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed proposer studies previous trajectories and hypotheses, writes candidate future harness programs; optional model-backed skill evolver derives generalizable process updates from cross-epoch failure/success patterns. - Decision owner: first-party configured proposer model (and optional second evolution model) for semantic adaptation options; deterministic validation/selection enforces the configured promotion criterion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: experience store, search/holdout split, validator/smoke/score ranking, staging/promoted code and versioned proposer instructions. - Closure path: sampled external task failures/traces → proposer diagnoses and writes candidate behavior changes → prospective evaluation on fresh/held-out tasks → S3 selection chooses a better parent → modified program becomes current harness for later tasks/epochs. - Boundary reachability: installed meta-agent loop directly calls invokeproposer, evaluates proposed files, and makes best candidate the parent for the next iteration. --evolve-skill optionally adds versioned proposer instruction evolution but is not needed for base S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides which novel changes in prompts, tools, stops, routing or code to propose from environmental evidence; Python scoring constrains acceptance but does not generate the future adaptation options. - External distinction: actual heterogeneous task outcomes, error traces and judge/label feedback reveal operating-environment demand beyond the proposer internal plan. - Future / prospective distinction: proposals modify the next harness capability and are tested on task cohorts/holdout before becoming the parent&#39;s future working configuration. - Adaptation option generated: alternative executable harness source, stopping criteria, tools/agent prompts and optional process-skill guidance. - Path back into current capability / S3: candidate validation/evaluation and best-parent selection feed the changed harness into the next epoch&#39;s installed operational program. - Evidence: README.md, loop/proposer.py (invokeproposer), loop/epoch.py (runoneepoch, runoneepochmulti), loop/skillevolver.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for prospective closed code path, not for measured improvement. - Caveats: adaptation is task-distribution/benchmark-bound and requires model/judge credentials; it does not imply awareness of arbitrary open-world future trends or proven transfer outside the selected benchmark.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-policy/identity deliberation/ratification cycle for the optimizer&#39;s mission or values. - Disturbance / variety regulated: acceptance thresholds, cost budgets, allowed edit surfaces and benchmark contracts constrain evolution but are preselected by the experiment operator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI and benchmark configs fix objective, proposer privileges, allowed edit surfaces and selection criterion; candidate evolution is explicitly prohibited from modifying target interfaces and benchmark truth. - Decision owner: external experimenter/maintainer through supplied configuration, not agentic ultimate authority or a parent-policy issue return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: surfacelock, staging validation, benchmark adapters, proposer instructions and run args. - Closure path: option checks enforce a fixed experimental mission; no system identity issue is escalated to legitimate policy authority and returned as a governing decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent editing prompts/harness code is adaptation/operation, not authority to define its organizational purpose/ultimate policy. - Evidence: loop/proposer.py, loop/validate.py, core/benchmark.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external organizational governance does not count without a first-party identity-level return-to-operation path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1d3ec2a5c696000d9ae3edc011f44bc4e1fae799/assessments/me/meta-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1d3ec2a5c696000d9ae3edc011f44bc4e1fae799\">1d3ec2a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:15:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/darshjme/djcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DJcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/darshjme/djcode/tree/d915b89db1b04cca88c2a495de355e4917a34b1a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d915b89db1b04cca88c2a495de355e4917a34b1a\">d915b89 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended software-engineering work against the selected project by inspecting artifacts, choosing tools/specialists, modifying code, running commands/tests and revising later actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repository structure, task ambiguity, file/Git state, tool and command results, provider failures, debugging/test outcomes, context limits and specialist findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next engineering tool/action or delegated specialist and determine when enough work/evidence exists to complete the task. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Operator, or a model-backed mutating specialist within its delegated operational scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/tool schemas; WorkflowEngine/DDAL dispatch; permissions/auto-accept; context manager; local tools; task tracker; specialist registry; sessions/memory; bounded tool rounds. - Closure path: task/current project context → model chooses action/tool → first-party dispatch/enforcement executes against the project → concrete result/error returns → model revises the next action or completes. - Boundary reachability: the ordinary CLI/TUI directly creates the Operator and exposes the full model/tool loop; auto-accept is a supported mode. Model-callable spawnagent is included in the normal provider tool schema and dispatch table. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, the deterministic workflow/permission/tool machinery cannot choose the task-specific engineering sequence or semantic code changes. - Evidence: agents/operator.py; provider.py; tools/init.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: approval is default for tool calls, but a supported authorized auto-accept mode provides the autonomous path; external providers supply inference only.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel mutating specialists can in principle collide in a shared workspace, but the reviewed distribution does not supply a worktree/file-ownership/lock/arbitration path that prevents or resolves those operational collisions and feeds the resolution back into later S1 behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic parallel/pipeline/wave scheduling; ContextBus locking/versioning; context handoff; DAF dependency scheduling; agent limits. - Closure path: not applicable. ContextBus can record duplicate-key conflicts, but no consumer uses the conflict list to arbitrate or alter later allocations/actions; shared-context propagation and DAG sequencing alone do not close S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: spawning many agents and passing outputs among them is topology/communication. The Methodology requires an evidenced interference-specific attenuation relation, which is absent for the actual mutating operational cells. - Evidence: agents/parallel.py; orchestrator/contextbus.py; workflow.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the ContextBus&#39;s “conflict” name is not sufficient; it detects information-key collisions but does not resolve them or control shared-workspace interference.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material discretionary whole-system current-control loop over the active operational population is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: routing, wave membership, concurrency, specialist timeouts, critical blocking gates, background-agent limits, workflow dependencies and command schedules are regulated, but chiefly by preset deterministic rules or task-local parent choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 scope. The main Operator can launch specialists and inspect background statuses, but no normal model-callable path was found to reprioritize, steer, reassign or cancel an existing multi-agent population using a whole-system current picture. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ShadowOrchestrator complexity/route/strategy rules; ParallelCoordinator; blocking-role gates; agent-status registry; fixed concurrency/nesting limits; DAF graph scheduler; explicit scheduler worker. - Closure path: preset routing/stage/gate rules can start/stop stages, but no whole-system current view → discretionary resource/commitment/prioritization judgment → changed live S1 commitments → returned operational-state loop is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-backed Vyasa profile describes orchestration behavior but is not the actor used by the normal ShadowOrchestrator.execute control path. Deterministic workflow control does not inherit S3 ownership from the label “orchestrator”. - Evidence: orchestrator/engine.py; agents/parallel.py; tools/agentspawn.py; scheduler.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model-backed blocking specialists can veto critical work, but that narrower assurance judgment is not a general whole-system S3 allocation/commitment owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an ordinary engineering result through a separately instantiated, role-restricted model reviewer that directly inspects project evidence and returns severity-tagged findings to the parent coding loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: correctness bugs, security flaws, performance/error-handling defects, insufficient tests, dependency problems and false-positive completion by the ordinary implementation actor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer child independently decides which observed conditions constitute review findings and their severity from its own read-only model/tool loop; the parent model receives that result and can revise the work or request another review. - Decision owner: the model-backed Reviewer (Dharma) child. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: normal model-facing spawnagent tool; specialist AgentRunner/AgentExecutor; separate ContextBus; Reviewer system prompt; read-only tool policy; foreground result return or background status/result channel; nesting/budget limits. - Closure path: main Operator implements/changes work → autonomously invokes spawnagent(role=&quot;reviewer&quot;, ...) → first-party runtime creates a separate reviewer model loop with direct read/search/Git access → reviewer returns file/line/severity findings as tool result → parent Operator receives the finding in its ordinary transcript → later model turns can fix/retest/review before completion. - Boundary reachability: spawnagent is in the normal provider tool schema and central dispatch, Reviewer is a built-in role, and children are instantiated by first-party code; no external Vyasa fleet or application-authored reviewer is required. Supported auto-accept allows this path without per-call human approval. - Claim being audited: that the parent/current implementation or recent project change is sufficiently correct, secure, performant and tested to be accepted as complete. - Ordinary reporting path: main Operator or mutating specialist&#39;s own tool results and completion response. - Complementary access path: a separately created Reviewer AgentRunner with its own context bus, role prompt and read-only direct repository tools re-inspects project state instead of relying only on the implementer&#39;s narrative. - Independence boundary: reviewer context/role/tool policy is distinct from the ordinary implementer and cannot mutate the workspace; the same underlying provider/model family may be reused, so independence is organizational/session/evidence-path separation rather than provider diversity. - Who acts on findings: the main Operator receives the reviewer response as a normal tool result and owns subsequent corrective code/test/delegation decisions; another reviewer invocation can challenge the revised state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the Reviewer model actor while keeping spawn plumbing and read-only enforcement removes the substantive defect/severity judgment; deterministic machinery cannot reproduce arbitrary code-review findings. - Evidence: provider.py; tools/agentspawn.py; agents/registry.py; agents/operator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: standalone user-invoked /review and deterministic /finish checks are not the basis of the autonomous claim; the positive path is the model-callable reviewer child returned to the active parent agent loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persisted memories, stored specialist results, user-created skills and current task routing can change later prompt context, but they do not form a first-party future-environment sensing → adaptation-option → enacted persistent capability change loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MemoryManager recall; ContextBus/vector result store and retrieval; skill loader/store; provider/model selection; roadmap/timeline and session persistence. - Closure path: prior results/memories are retrieved as later context, and user-created skill files can be loaded, but no standard autonomous path decides to create/adopt a future capability in response to prospective environmental intelligence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable experience reuse is memory, and skill creation is a user/project configuration surface. Neither demonstrates the distinct outside-and-then adaptation function. - Evidence: orchestrator/engine.py; skills.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future runs can benefit from stored context; persistence alone is not prospective organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity or ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, tool policies, permissions, custom agent/team definitions and blocking rules constrain operation, but no first-party actor adjudicates an identity/ultimate-policy issue and returns that decision as authoritative governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5 scope. - Decision owner: not established inside the runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: main/specialist system prompts; permission manager and auto-accept; agent specs; user-defined Project Studio agents/organisations/flows; config; safety/approval rules. - Closure path: configuration and prompts are supplied/edited externally and then enforced or loaded. No runtime identity-policy issue is escalated to legitimate ultimate authority, decided there and returned to govern subsequent organization-wide operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the primary/specialist agents act inside authored policy; neither they nor deterministic orchestration have authority to redefine DJcode&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity/policy. - Evidence: prompt.py; agents/registry.py; permissions.py; studio.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: custom organizations and approval rules can be durable, but authored configuration is not runtime S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e8b5b127d14fcaaeed6f07855d16ae72aa8c2024/assessments/dj/djcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e8b5b127d14fcaaeed6f07855d16ae72aa8c2024\">e8b5b12 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:21:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dxiv/dxa-deimos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Deimos</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dxiv/dxa-deimos/tree/933a20c064294cb84dafaae2fe0dae617eb44c48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 933a20c064294cb84dafaae2fe0dae617eb44c48\">933a20c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue uncertain coding outcomes through iterative model-selected file, shell, search and other first-party tool actions, including individual teammate tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete coding instructions, new source state, command/tool outcomes, model/provider errors, changing team work and feedback from other agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next tool/operation or completion after observing the previous tool/agent result. - Decision owner: a configured local/cloud inference model operating in a first-party Deimos query/agent-tool loop; external API only supplies inference and transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK adapter and provider profiles, tool dispatcher, terminal session state, permission gates, context handling, subprocess/file tools and model selection. - Closure path: user or team assignment → model request → selected first-party action/tool → tool output → next model turn/operation or completion returned to user/team lead. - Boundary reachability: the public CLI launcher loads the built native Deimos entrypoint; the own-source query engine and AgentTool produce action/feedback cycles. Team spawning is reachable from the documented first-party optional mode and not from an adjacent hosted agent alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model while leaving API adapters and tools in place removes the context-sensitive decision about coding actions; static runtime mechanisms cannot replace the operational choice. - Evidence: bin/deimos.mjs; src/entrypoints/cli.tsx; src/query.ts; src/QueryEngine.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/AgentTool/runAgent.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is evidence for native decision-right mapping, not live benchmark accuracy or successful packaging of every optional tool.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate actual contention and premature succession between distinct active operational teammates through shared task-ownership conflict checks and dependency admission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent attempts by two teammates to claim one task, overcommitment of a busy agent, and a successor task begun before its dependencies finish. - Decisive decision or feedback right: file-lock-serialized, owner/dependency/busy-state checked admission or rejection of a claim; result controls whether a given S1 may start the task. - Decision owner: native deterministic first-party coordination constructor; teammate models may select subsequent eligible work but no separately evidenced autonomous agent owns the conflict-specific arbitration policy. Hence C, not automatic A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proper-lockfile, disk task list, owner/blockedBy graph, watcher, in-process runner, TaskUpdate and mailbox status. - Closure path: teammate sees an unclaimed task → tries claimTask → exclusive lock plus owner/blocked/busy checks → refusal or approved claim returned to watcher/runner → only accepted worker begins its operational prompt; blocked or contending agents wait or inspect newly available work. - Distinct S1 units: two named first-party team workers handling separate repository modifications under a single team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous ownership claims and incompatible active dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party atomic locking and task dependency checks, not merely a shared mailbox or generic queue. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: task-list watcher/in-process runner uses returned success/failure to select or defer its next prompt; failure to submit releases the claim. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implemented relation directly identifies and rejects a concrete conflict between independent operating agents before work proceeds, and delivers that arbitration into the operational selection loop. - Boundary reachability: native DEIMOSEXPERIMENTALAGENTTEAMS/flag opt-in enables TeamCreate and teammate spawn; the shipped useTaskListWatcher and inProcessRunner actually call the same src/utils/tasks.ts claimTask method. No external runtime is needed for the collision rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic lock arbitration remains even if agentic reasoning is removed; it establishes constructed S2 conflict regulation, not model-owned S2 policy. - Evidence: src/utils/tasks.ts; src/hooks/useTaskListWatcher.ts; src/utils/swarm/inProcessRunner.ts; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: direct manual TaskUpdate ownership editing is distinct from the protected claimTask code; the positive relation is the runtime automatic-claim path, not a universal no-conflicts guarantee.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain cohesion over an opted-in current team by assigning, revising and supervising several ongoing operational obligations and available teammates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked dependent work, idle or overburdened teammates, unexpected subtask failures, stale commitments and competing priorities in the active team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-directed team lead may choose team formation, current owner/priority allocation, updated dependencies, interventions to unblock work, instructions to teammates and shutdown/reallocation of the current team. - Decision owner: first-party team-lead model at the local team recursion in supported opt-in mode; task persistence and mailbox delivery merely enforce/communicate its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamCreate/team file/roster, TaskList/TaskUpdate, SendMessage, worker status and shared task metadata. - Whole-system current view: the team-lead can inspect the complete team task list with owner/status/blocker information and receive teammate completion/blocked or idle messages, rather than merely the contents of one operative tool call. - Current-control decision scope: priority/ownership among several currently operative teammates, resource allocation and intervention through task ownership and communication. - Closure path: teammate blocks/completes or signals difficulty → lead reads whole-team state/messages → model chooses reassignment/dependency adjustment/worker instruction → TaskUpdate/SendMessage records/delivers decision → subsequent teammate activity changes. - Boundary reachability: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts initializes native team lead and shared task list in the enabled first-party mode; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts and src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts expose ongoing obligations and discretionary owner/dependency changes; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts returns consequential directions to native teammates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic task store cannot decide what current team obligations should be changed in response to evolving progress; the model controls this whole-current choice rather than only forwarding messages. - Evidence: src/tools/TeamCreateTool/TeamCreateTool.ts; src/tools/TeamCreateTool/prompt.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; src/tools/TaskListTool/TaskListTool.ts; src/tools/TaskUpdateTool/TaskUpdateTool.ts; src/tools/SendMessageTool/SendMessageTool.ts; src/utils/swarm/teamHelpers.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: only the explicitly opt-in team mode supplies whole-current control; default single coder and user approvals are not re-labelled as S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no enabled independent complementary operational-evidence review with a consequential findings-return loop is established in the open Deimos distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source changes and task-completion claims may be wrong, but routine local reviews/tests and maintainer CI do not establish this additional organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor would inspect distinct primary artifacts, judge contradictory evidence and inform current control; the frozen open build instead disables the named specialized verifier and has no completed native alternative audit path. - Decision owner: no positive native S3 owner established. Internal verifier code exists as an adjacent disabled feature; the main coding agent controls ordinary local review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local /review prompt and /security-review, test/code tools, optional external /ultrareview, and maintainer CodeQL/CI—all outside a first-party independent complementary audit close. - Closure path: ordinary coder may read source or tests and continue S1; no alternative first-party auditor with separate judgment and a returned metasystemic control correction is operationally established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: verificationAgent presence is not sufficient when build-time feature(&#39;VERIFICATIONAGENT&#39;) is false and runtime GrowthBook gate defaults false, and a local model requested to review its own work is not an independent complementary audit by name. - Evidence: scripts/build.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/builtInAgents.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/verificationAgent.ts; src/tools/VerifyPlanExecutionTool/VerifyPlanExecutionTool.ts; src/tools/ReviewArtifactTool/ReviewArtifactTool.ts; src/commands/review.ts; src/commands/security-review.ts; .github/workflows/codeql.yml. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the frozen open-build boundary. - Caveats: this does not assert that developer CI, an internal build, or a separately hosted external service lacks review/audit. Their decision rights are not credited to this assessed publicly shipped CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed externally prospective adaptation mechanism that generates future capability options and returns them into current S3 oversight was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latency/cost changes among inference providers, new task and repository context and externally hosted service capabilities are addressed by operational routing/configuration rather than a prospective organizational intelligence cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured Python provider sidecar may select a current backend by observed health/latency; user/provider profiles govern model selection. This is not a future-directed adaptation judgment over the team&#39;s capabilities. - Decision owner: no evidenced first-party S4 at the local installed coding/agent-team recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: python/smartrouter.py experimental sidecar, provider bootstrap/discovery, model choice and task planning; PROACTIVE=false and AGENTTRIGGERS=false in scripts/build.ts. - Closure path: current model/backend switch informs the next inference request, not a future-state/options decision returned into S3 control to transform longer-run capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider health scoring and context-dependent code plan are operational, not environmental foresight; future-looking labels on disabled features cannot supply an implemented S4 loop. - Evidence: python/smartrouter.py; scripts/provider-recommend.ts; scripts/build.ts; src/commands/ultraplan.tsx; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately managed user organization may assess future models and alter its installation, but that external governance is not native S4 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-level identity/ultimate policy decision path is established for this assessed local agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, model/provider choice, tool permissions, usage limits and opt-in team access are operational constraints rather than conflicts about organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/developers choose settings and approve particular tools, while the runtime enforces fixed rules; no identity/policy choice closing S3–S4 tension is observed. - Decision owner: no native ultimate-policy owner; external human or maintainer may govern their broader project independently. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config/profile editor, local permission gates, policy-limit helpers, source build flags and team mode enablement. - Closure path: a local tool action is approved/rejected or a mode toggled and coding continues; no higher-order identity issue → legitimate policy authority → binding returned system-operation constraint is wired as a first-party S5 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static source-build policy and local operator approvals are not an autonomous S5 decision or a legitimate parent-mode ultimate-policy return. - Evidence: src/services/policyLimits/index.ts; src/utils/agentSwarmsEnabled.ts; scripts/build.ts; src/tools/AgentTool/AgentTool.tsx; README.md; LEGAL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organizational/legal governance recorded in public docs is adjacent, not automatically an operating parent mode of the Deimos CLI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bd599e834eea44b568ccea9819d5e49adbf7061f/assessments/deimos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bd599e834eea44b568ccea9819d5e49adbf7061f\">bd599e8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:25:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/525300887039/BareAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BareAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/525300887039/BareAgent/tree/1dfd1057d372e872f54c0a666f375dce2bc11a26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1dfd1057d372e872f54c0a666f375dce2bc11a26\">1dfd105 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - Function: Autonomously perform uncertain developer coding tasks through file/shell/tool actions and observed feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository state, errors, test failures, unavailable resources, and follow-up developer questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root model or each teammate model chooses which action to execute and how to respond to the returned result. - State: A - Decision owner: First-party model-driven root/teammate agent loops; the provider supplies inference while the built-in runtime carries out local actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed tool schemas/handlers, hooks/permissions, retry policy, message persistence/compaction, shell and file tools, background execution. - Closure path: Work request → model-selected action → tool/handler result → model feedback in the same coding loop → next action or final result; background teammate handles a message or claimed task using the same loop. - Boundary reachability: Default REPL invokes native agentloop; the documented /team spawn path creates independent running AutonomousAgent workers that invoke agentloop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool execution and permissions do not pick a context-appropriate coding action without the operative model. - Evidence: core/loop.py; main.py; autonomous.py; tools.py. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - Function: Attenuate duplicate execution of one shared pending task when several autonomous teammate S1 workers see the same ready item. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running teammates can concurrently select the same pending task before either finishes; duplicate claims/work would waste work or conflict on the same task commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The shared task manager compares a task&#39;s expected status against current status and atomically commits the first claim; a rejected claimant re-evaluates the ready set rather than executing that task. - State: C - Decision owner: First-party deterministic claim-and-retry constraint; a developer/agent must still supply any discretionary policy for arbitration or rescheduling beyond first-winner conflict attenuation. No S2 coordinating agent owns adaptive arbitration as shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager RLock, expectedstatus compare-and-update, persisted .tasks.json, dependency readiness, per-teammate run loops. - Closure path: Concurrent ready-task observation → first atomic pending→inprogress update → competing update raises ValueError → second teammate skips the lost claim and searches other ready tasks instead. - Boundary reachability: TaskManager instance is shared by the main REPL and spawned AutonomousAgent workers through maketeamhandlers; workers invoke claimtask for ready work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision-sensitive response is embedded in explicit first-party code but chosen by lock/CAS rules, not by a model&#39;s independent coordination judgment, so it supports C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: Separately spawned AutonomousAgent background threads, each with its own provider, prompt, handler set, and task execution loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing acquisition of the same ready persisted task by two running teammate agents. - Attenuating coordination relation: Compare-and-set claim on shared task status and losing-claim rejection within the worker claim loop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Losing worker does not execute a task it failed to claim; it resumes scan or waits, while winning worker marks done/failed on completion. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This path rejects a concrete competing claim by two operating workers and changes which worker may proceed; the generic message bus, team routing and static workflow DAG are not used as independent S2 evidence. - Evidence: tasks.py update/getreadytasks; AutonomousAgent claim loop; shared TaskManager wiring. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - Function: Own whole-current commitments and interventions for a live team of independently executing coding S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple running teammate commitments, pending/done/failed tasks and developer requests can require reassignment, roster adjustment, work reprioritization, shutdown, or additional work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In agent mode the root model may inspect task and team rosters, create/update tasks, register/spawn/shutdown workers, and send targeted work requests based on responses. In separate parent mode the human does equivalent current intervention via /team and task command surfaces. - State: A(P) - Decision owner: Autonomous root model in first-party agent-tool mode; human operator for the separately exposed command-controlled parent mode. Background manager and TaskManager transport/enforce these decisions but do not autonomously choose current priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamlist, teamregister, teamspawn, teamsend, teamshutdown; tasklist/taskcreate/taskupdate; per-session MessageBus and background worker state; running-thread probe; reply-to-request protocol. - Closure path: Root agent sees whole-team roster/task status and teammate results → chooses new current allocation or intervention → concrete team/task tool action → updated task commitments, active teammate population, or returned worker instruction → subsequent worker execution/feedback. - Boundary reachability: Team and task tool schemas are standard first-party tools registered through core/tools.py and connected by buildhandlers/maketeamhandlers in main.py. /team commands address the same handlers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The root model can decide when to change team composition, who handles a request, or which commitments to create. A deterministic scheduler only enforces claims; a human alternative exists without removing autonomous tool-mode choice. - Whole-system current view: teamlist returns all registered workers with actual running status; tasklist returns current work records and statuses; teamsend and mailbox responses provide subordinate results. - Current-control decision scope: Create/change a task commitment, launch/stop a worker, and send/revise an operational request across a whole current team; this is not solely selecting a single child function call. - Evidence: TEAMTOOLSCHEMAS and task tools; team handlers and /team commands; worker task behavior; task ledger. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium: meaningful current-control reachability is clear; a live multi-worker strategy adaptation trace has not been independently reproduced - Caveats: Default solo mode is only one S1 and does not exercise a team-level S3. Positive S3 is credited to the documented first-party team mode, not the bare presence of a queue or generic model planning.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - Function: No operationally guaranteed complementary independent audit of claims about teammate execution with a return to whole-current control was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A teammate may produce faulty work or a weak plan; ordinary teamrequestreview solicits a model&#39;s opinion on the plan, not a verified independent observation of what the worker actually did. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The root model or human requests a review; a nominated teammate writes an answer. The standard PLANAPPROVAL path does not require sampled operational artifacts or separate protected evidence before an audit verdict is returned. - State: — - Decision owner: Requested reviewer model for plan opinion and the requester for next action; no distinct source-first operational audit authority guaranteed by shipped contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProtocolFSM PLANAPPROVAL request/response, separate provider-backed teammate, shared read-capable tools, per-request prompt wrapper, optional tests and traces. - Closure path: Requester-supplied plan → teammate review prompt → textual verdict → requester. Missing guaranteed independent operational evidence → audit judgment → whole-current management correction circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent worker identity alone is not sufficient for S3; normal plan approval does not establish sufficiently complementary access to operational reality. - Evidence: teamrequestreview; buildincomingprompt; plan approval protocol; team docs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - Function: No first-party external-and-prospective option-modeling function with feedback into whole-current capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project knowledge, user demands or reusable workflows may affect future work, but first-party skill reflection captures previous local actions and user-promoted drafts rather than prospectively modeling external change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reflection model may propose a reusable skill; the human decides whether to promote; neither step demonstrates a distinct outside/future intelligence decision coupled to S3 current control. - State: — - Decision owner: Current coding model for task learning/drafts, operator for promotion; no attributable S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SkillGenerator two-threshold trigger, isolated reflection model run, pending skill store, /skill keep promotion, model/provider configuration. - Closure path: Accumulated local work → LLM reflects and drafts skill → human promotes into later skill catalog. Missing independent externally sensed change, prospective option appraisal, and return into current team strategy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic experiential learning or reusable procedures do not satisfy the Profile&#39;s external-and-future condition by themselves. - Evidence: skillgen.py; runskillreflection and promotion; skill catalog. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - Function: No first-party ultimate organizational identity/constitutional policy arbitration loop is evidenced at the live coding team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission limits, developer config, workspace rules and team role descriptions constrain actions but do not resolve ultimate purpose/policy conflicts for the team organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human config sets security permissions/roles; root agent may define teammate work roles. Neither is a proven ultimate identity-policy authority with return to the operating whole. - State: — - Decision owner: Human for local permissions and safety preferences; deterministic enforcement for tool operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionGuard, team role/system-prompt registry, sandbox, approval hooks, config and fail-closed child permissions. - Closure path: User sets local constraint → permission or role handler applies it to a tool/worker. No S3–S4 identity conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy decision → whole-team return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task permissions, system prompts and teammate role selection remain execution governance, not proof of constitutive identity decisions. - Evidence: permissions; teammate roles; team composition; hooks. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f07cb3f6ee281940aa69e93dd7a06b494ec31be8/assessments/bareagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f07cb3f6ee281940aa69e93dd7a06b494ec31be8\">f07cb3f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T09:09:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/haorui-harry/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/haorui-harry/agent-harness/tree/109dde248cb44227a9ed3a204790432a6da843da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 109dde248cb44227a9ed3a204790432a6da843da\">109dde2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an open-ended user request into concrete workspace/tool/evidence actions and a delivered artifact through a persistent thread execution whose operational plan can be model-selected and revised from execution feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, available capabilities, current workspace state, tool/evidence results, failed or missing artifacts/validation, and changing completion gaps during the run. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in live-enabled mode, choose the substantive task-graph actions to perform and revise those actions after observing current execution state and completion gaps. - Decision owner: the configured live model actor reached through first-party task-profile expansion/subagent/replan prompts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessEngine, task-profile compiler, executable graph/dependencies, TaskGraphActionMapper, tool/skill registries, persistent thread/workspace state, command/tool execution, safety constraints and model-call budgets. - Closure path: user request → thread/profile/graph context → live model selects operational graph additions/actions → runtime executes tools/workspace/subagent work → results/artifacts/world state enter noderesults/completion state → live replan can select additional actions → final synthesis/delivery reflects the changed operational state. - Boundary reachability: ThreadFirstSuperAgent.run() is a shipped standard entrypoint; it invokes compilegenerictaskpayload(...), passes the supported livemodel configuration, and executes the resulting graph through the credited thread runtime without requiring an adjacent development/evaluation system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the live model from the live-enabled mode removes the discretionary task-specific choice of model-generated graph additions/replan actions; deterministic fallback machinery remains, but it does not make materially the same open-ended decisions with the same discretion. - Evidence: app/harness/superagent.py; app/harness/taskprofile.py; app/agents/taskactions.py; app/agents/runtime.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: no-key mode has substantially more deterministic planning. A is credited to the supported live-enabled first-party operating mode, not to every execution configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination function is established at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate disturbances inspected included concurrent subagent/shared-workspace interference, skill-output conflict/redundancy, graph scheduling dependencies and parallel mission-track execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a specific disturbance among distinct S1 operational units. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task-graph dependency ordering, sequential skill execution, complementarity/conflict scoring, conflict/consensus reports, persistent shared thread state, and the optional parallel execution helper. - Closure path: no qualifying disturbance → coordination response → subsequent S1 behavior loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S2 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/agents/subagents.py; app/main.py; app/routing/complementarity.py; app/routing/executor.py; app/graph.py. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the currently executing job as a whole when present commitments no longer close the task, by deciding which additional operational commitments should be inserted before delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing required artifacts/channels/validation, command or tool failure, evidence/workspace gaps, incomplete current execution, and mismatches between the task specification and observed world state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose new current commitments from workspaceaction, toolcall, and subagent actions after seeing whole-job execution/completion state, and insert those commitments into the active graph. - Decision owner: the configured live model actor in livereplansuggestions() for the live-enabled mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic task/world-state construction, failure-policy classification, capability registry suggestions, fallback repair seed, node allow-lists, graph mutation, dependency rewiring, persistent execution state, scheduler/recovery machinery and tool/workspace enforcement. - Closure path: current graph executes → completion packet/world state exposes unresolved gap → graphreplan receives current job state → live model selects additional commitments → runtime appends the selected nodes and rewires synthesis/completion dependencies → subsequent execution performs the added work before the job can close. - Boundary reachability: the standard task-profile compiler adds a first-party graphreplan node whenever graph expansion enables replanning, and the standard thread-first execution path reaches TaskGraphActionMapper.executegraphreplan; supported livemodel configuration is carried through the same runtime context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the live model, the runtime can still apply deterministic fallback/capability repairs, but it no longer makes the same task-specific discretionary choice among allowed new commitments. The model therefore owns the open-ended current-control decision in the credited mode; the runtime enforces and persists it. - Evidence: app/harness/taskprofile.py; app/agents/taskactions.py; app/agents/runtime.py; app/harness/superagent.py; tests/testagentruntime.py as corroboration. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: S3 is mode-dependent; the autonomous owner exists in live-model-enabled execution. Deterministic recovery/scheduling alone would not justify A. - Whole-system current view: replan input includes query/graph id, failure policy, completion packet, stategap, capability replan, accumulated noderesults, execution-loop schema, current loop phase, phase summary and fallback seed; this is a job-wide current-state view rather than one worker&#39;s local result. - Current-control decision scope: add or withhold new tool calls, workspace actions and bounded subagent commitments, and thereby alter the active graph dependencies that gate synthesis/completion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary operational-audit path is established in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate audit surfaces inspected included the live critique pass, structured dissent/conflict verification, completion/validation nodes, traces, red-team/evaluator utilities, tests and report artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no complementary audit judgment with materially different access to operational reality is closed at the assessed runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critique prompts, evidence digest, deterministic conflict/dissent checks, validation artifacts, traces and development/evaluation utilities. - Closure path: not established for an independent audit channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S3 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/harness/liveagent.py; app/graph.py; app/routing/executor.py; adjacent tests/ and evaluator/red-team surfaces. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no organizational outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the assessed deployment boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate surfaces inspected included external evidence/search, runtime learning statistics, optimizer runs, live experiments, research-lab comparisons, strategy selection and skill reliability feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was found that turns external/future-relevant distinctions into organizational adaptation options and returns those options into present harness capability. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evidence providers, recordrun learning statistics, reliability-aware skill selection, optimizer/experiment result stores and research-lab tooling. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S4 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/memory/learning.py; app/routing/complementarity.py; app/harness/optimizer.py; app/harness/iteration.py; evidence/search paths in app/harness/evidence.py. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate surfaces inspected included system modes, risk/governance policy bundles, human approval/interrupt controls, constraints, recipes and static runtime settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was found for resolving an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue and returning that authoritative decision to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static PolicyBundle values, runtime constraints, safety checks, approval flags, operator interrupt/resume and configuration. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ownership is not classified because the S5 function itself is not established. - Evidence: app/policy/center.py; runtime constraints/settings in app/harness/models.py and app/harness/runtimesettings.py; thread control in app/agents/runtime.py. - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f2b0fec6e23af8b058420f9cbad438f21ebcc4ab/assessments/haorui-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f2b0fec6e23af8b058420f9cbad438f21ebcc4ab\">f2b0fec &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T18:54:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/open-multi-agent/open-multi-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OMA / Open Multi-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/open-multi-agent/open-multi-agent/tree/4107fb144653c3240c569f345cf06b028dd7747a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4107fb144653c3240c569f345cf06b028dd7747a\">4107fb1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute assigned goals/tasks against model/tool environments and produce operational outcomes for the caller/run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous prompts and task requirements, model responses, tool/API results, dependency payloads, context state, structured-output failures and external-backend results requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next model-mediated action within a task — respond, invoke an allowed tool, consume observations, continue reasoning/delegation where enabled, and settle the task result. - Decision owner: the active OMA built-in LLM worker agent for standard built-in-agent modes; external process/ACP workers own their private inner loops and are not imported as first-party OMA decision actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent runner, model adapters, tool registry/grants, AgentPool, task queue, dependency/context injection, shared memory, budgets/timeouts, checkpoints and run-store fencing. - Closure path: task/prompt + current context → agent model selects action/tool → runtime executes/returns observation → observation re-enters agent context → agent selects further action or final result → task/run settlement and dependent release. - Boundary reachability: runAgent, runTasks and runTeam with built-in agents are first-party documented @open-multi-agent/core operating modes, not examples or CI-only paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven worker while leaving the scheduler, queue, stores, approval machinery and tool executor leaves no actor that makes the contextual task-level operational choices. Runtime machinery constrains and persists those decisions but does not substitute for them. - Evidence: packages/core/README.md, README.md, task-scheduling.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination loop was established for a concrete interference/oscillation among distinct OMA S1 worker units at the declared run/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate disturbances inspected included parallel ready tasks, competition for AgentPool capacity, duplicate processes advancing one logical run, dependency ordering and shared-memory writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific autonomous coordination discretion is supplied. Scheduler assignment/load balancing is whole-run resource/commitment control; run-store fencing prevents duplicate ownership of the same run; dependency edges sequence work; namespaced shared memory avoids key collisions. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPool semaphore, task dependencies, event-driven ready set, least-busy/composite scheduling, namespaced shared memory, run-store lease/fencing and deterministic task-status propagation. - Closure path: no first-party S2-specific feedback loop was found that observes an interaction disturbance among independent S1 workers and changes their subsequent local behaviour to damp it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple agents and parallel branches exist, but task assignment, dependency sequencing and capacity limits are not S2 merely because they coordinate execution. The reviewed mechanisms map more directly to S3 resource/current-control or duplicate-execution safety and do not expose an S2-specific autonomous decision path. - Evidence: task-scheduling.md, scheduler.ts, run-store.md, shared-memory.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments and interventions after operational outcomes reveal that the pending task graph should change. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failure, consensus rejection, successful outcomes that invalidate/reshape downstream work, assignee infeasibility and remaining budget/commitment constraints while other pending/blocked work still exists. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to keep the current pending graph or propose a PlanPatch that adds replacement tasks, retargets pending tasks or supersedes pending branches in response to a current task outcome. - Decision owner: OMA does not supply the autonomous decisive replanner. The developer/application supplies a Replanner or onTaskOutcome actor; that actor may itself call an LLM/service, but such autonomous composition remains application-owned. OMA owns validation, atomic application and execution closure after a proposal exists. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskOutcome snapshot of complete current task state and remaining budgets, deterministic Scheduler and AgentSelector, recovery limits, DAG validation, optional onPlanPatch gate, checkpoint persistence and event-driven publication of newly-ready work. - Closure path: task settles/reaches verification outcome → OMA constructs whole-run TaskOutcome → composed replanner proposes patch → OMA validates/gates and atomically applies accepted current-control changes → new/retargeted pending work enters the same scheduler and changes subsequent execution. - Whole-system current view: TaskOutcome.tasks exposes the full current task list together with current plan revision, triggering task/result/verification and remaining budget dimensions; scheduler decisions also inspect current DAG/load state. - Current-control decision scope: pending commitments may be appended, retargeted to another eligible agent, or superseded, thereby changing who will perform current work and which remaining commitments still count toward the run. - Boundary reachability: repairable recovery, Replanner, onTaskOutcome, PlanPatch validation and atomic application are documented first-party @open-multi-agent/core runtime surfaces reachable in runTasks/team execution; the missing element is the developer-composed decisive actor, which is why the state is C, not A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic scheduling already enforces current assignment/criticality/load policy, but no shipped autonomous actor owns the discretionary replan judgment. The first-party recovery seam is S3-specific and operationally closes a composed decision back into current execution, satisfying the narrow constructor threshold without pretending the scheduler itself is an autonomous S3 owner. - Evidence: adaptive-recovery.md, recovery.ts, task-scheduling.md, scheduler.ts. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the ordinary one-shot coordinator is not this S3 owner; it is explicitly absent mid-run. Durable plan/dispatch approval can constrain current work but OMA deliberately leaves reviewer product/transport/authorization to the integrator, so no separate parent-governed S3 mode is published here.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a proposed operational result and feed an audit judgment back into the result/control path before downstream use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but incorrect/incomplete task answers whose ordinary worker completion path would otherwise become accepted downstream state without an independent challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent judge agents decide accept/dissent against the original question and proposed answer; quorum and configured dissent policy determine whether the result is accepted, rejected or returned to the proposer for revision. - Decision owner: the configured OMA judge-agent roster owns the audit judgments; deterministic quorum parsing/aggregation and runtime gating transport those judgments. On onDissent: &#39;revise&#39;, the proposer remains the operational actor producing the corrected candidate, while judges retain the complementary audit right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runConsensus, per-task verify, separate judge invocations, structured {accept, critique} verdicts, quorum/max-round controls, dissent persistence, token/cost accounting, shared-memory verdict records and task-output replacement after an accepted revision. - Closure path: worker/proposer produces candidate → separate judges receive original question + candidate and issue verdicts/critiques → quorum/dissent policy resolves audit → dissent can be fed back to proposer for another round → an accepted revised answer supersedes task output and is propagated into queue/shared memory/downstream results. - Claim being audited: that the proposer/worker&#39;s candidate adequately answers the original task/question and should be accepted as the task result. - Ordinary reporting path: the worker/proposer&#39;s own final answer/result through the ordinary task completion path. - Complementary access path: distinct judge-agent invocations independently evaluate the candidate against the original question under refute or differentiated-lens prompts; they are not the worker&#39;s ordinary completion check and can use separately configured models/agents. - Independence boundary: judge agents are a separately configured verifier roster, invoked after/around the proposer result with their own model calls and structured verdict contract; they receive the candidate rather than inheriting the proposer&#39;s hidden reasoning state. Different judges can use different models and lens framings. The classification does not rely on generic traces, governance receipts or the post-run evaluation subsystem. - Who acts on findings: the first-party consensus loop aggregates judge decisions; under the default revise policy it returns dissent to the proposer, and per-task verification replaces downstream task state only when a revised candidate is accepted. - Boundary reachability: runConsensus() is a documented top-level core runtime mode and verify is a documented per-task hook in runTasks/runTeam; both are shipped first-party runtime paths rather than CI/eval-only machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the judge agents while leaving quorum code, traces and result plumbing removes the substantive independent audit judgments. Deterministic parsing and quorum enforcement cannot recreate critiques or accept/dissent decisions, so the decisive S3 feedback is agent-owned. - Evidence: consensus.md, coordinator.md, packages/core/README.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: governance-floor receipts and journal verification are useful audit mechanisms but are not the reason for A; post-run evaluation explicitly does not alter the business result. The positive mapping is the independent judge loop with corrective return.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime path was established that senses external/future-relevant change, develops adaptation options and returns a strategic capability change into present OMA operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate S4 inputs inspected included provider/tool failures, task outcomes, evaluation regressions/trends, routing profiles, model/provider choices and changing external requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime S4 adaptation judgment is supplied. Adaptive recovery changes the current unexecuted graph in response to current task outcomes (S3-constructor scope), while the evaluation subsystem measures completed runs without changing their business result. - Decision owner: none established for S4 in the assessed runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: offline/online evaluation reports and gates, model/execution routing, adaptive recovery, configuration, callbacks, provider fallback and application-owned policy code. - Closure path: no supported first-party outside/prospective sensing → option-generation → adaptation-decision → changed present capability loop was found inside the product-runtime boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluation can expose versions, regressions and trends, but its documented contract is observe-only relative to business runs; using its reports to change prompts/models/topology in CI or operator practice is an adjacent application/development loop unless separately wired by the integrator. - Evidence: evaluation.md, adaptive-recovery.md, packages/core/README.md. - Basis: explicit -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy escalation-decision-return loop was established at the OMA run/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate policy matters included declared governance roles, consequential-tool rules, egress policy, approval decisions, role/tool boundaries, budget ceilings and explicit topology overrides. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or operationally closed first-party parent authority resolves identity/ultimate-policy disputes for the system. Applications declare governance intent, tool grants, egress/budget policy and reviewer integration before/around execution. - Decision owner: application/developer/organization outside the assessed runtime owns ultimate policy; OMA deterministically validates/enforces selected rules and records deviations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: governanceIntent/required roles/order, execution receipts and governanceConclusion, consequential-tool classification/confirmation, egress policy, tool allow/deny lists, budgets, durable approval ledger, run-store cancellation and configuration. - Closure path: configured constraints and approval decisions can change/stop subsequent execution, but no first-party standard path carries an identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that authority&#39;s decision as S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: governance receipts prove that declared roles/order executed; they do not decide what the organization&#39;s identity or ultimate policy should be. Durable approvals bind exact reviewed content but OMA deliberately provides no reviewer product, routing, RBAC or escalation layer. These are strong enforcement/governance primitives without an S5 decision owner. - Evidence: tool-configuration.md, durable-approvals.md, observability.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: an organization can build a legitimate parent-policy layer on top of the durable approval/governance primitives; Methodology 0.3.5 does not publish S5 merely because a human can approve an operational action or because policy text is enforceable.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b4ce8b5a5cb50fb5ea1941fc7e0a2e73781ec6ea/assessments/open-multi-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b4ce8b5a5cb50fb5ea1941fc7e0a2e73781ec6ea\">b4ce8b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T01:59:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonnyparris/dodo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dodo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonnyparris/dodo/tree/156d60ca2f743ad4177b0ed781e61ecbd1af508f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 156d60ca2f743ad4177b0ed781e61ecbd1af508f\">156d60c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out repository-oriented coding, investigation and delivery work through first-party model/tool sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases, tool outcomes, browser/repository evidence, task goals, context pressure, repeated tool loops, test failures, scheduled execution and human-input blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool/action, interpret results, continue across turns under a goal, and decide when the requested operational outcome is done/blocked/needs input. - Decision owner: the model-backed CodingAgent or goal-driven autopilot worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Durable Object session, workspace isolation, Git/browser/code tools, doom-loop guard, token budget, compaction, scheduler, goal state and setgoalstatus. - Closure path: task/goal + context → model selects action → tool/environment returns evidence → model revises work → goal/session terminal or continued turn. - Boundary reachability: ordinary UI/MCP sessions and goal-driven/scheduled modes are shipped first-party paths at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves storage/scheduling/guardrails but removes open-ended coding and investigation decisions. - Evidence: README; docs/session-goals.md; CodingAgent/agentic runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Git hosting and model inference are dependencies, not decision owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying same-recursion peer-S1 disturbance/coordination loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: batch-dispatched sessions have separate workspaces; facets can run concurrently and task facets can use scratch prefixes; supervisor deduplication allocates work from above. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct S2 mutual-adjustment decision among peer operational units is established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session workspace isolation, scratch workspaces, task/facet naming, batch-dispatch caps and supervisor duplicate suppression. - Closure path: isolation prevents state collision, but no concrete peer disturbance is sensed and resolved through a coordination relation that changes both/peer S1 behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the strongest concurrency mechanisms are partitioning/delegation or S3 allocation, not a separate S2 decision right. - Evidence: README task-board/facet sections; docs/facets.md; task dispatch routes. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: if a future first-party flow explicitly reconciles competing session changes against one shared delivery surface, this finding could change.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current Dodo maintenance activity in the shipped autopilot mode by deciding which live/recent failures merit investigation and allocating bounded worker sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker exceptions, client-error groups, stalled scheduled sessions, duplicate/in-flight investigations, repeated failure patterns and bounded worker capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether there is a concrete actionable issue, which issue(s) to investigate now, whether an issue is already covered, and whether to dispatch up to three worker sessions or pause/escalate a repeated pattern. - Decision owner: the model-backed scheduled autopilot supervisor session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listfailedsessions, listautopilotworkers, dispatchautopilotworker, cron scheduling, worker goal state, max-three rule and ntfy notification. - Closure path: cross-section failure evidence + prior worker state → supervisor judgment → new goal-driven worker dispatch or no-dispatch/pause → workers update status/open draft PRs → later supervisor run reads worker/failure state and adjusts subsequent dispatch. - Whole-system current view: the supervisor consumes a cross-section of Worker exceptions, grouped client errors, stalled scheduled sessions and the current/recent autopilot-worker roster for the deployed Dodo instance. - Current-control decision scope: it chooses whether current failures merit intervention, which concrete issues receive scarce worker slots now, suppresses duplicate/in-flight work, and pauses/escalates repeated stuck patterns. - Boundary reachability: README documents installation of the scheduled supervisor; src/index.ts exposes the supervisor schedule; src/mcp.ts ships the exact control tools; src/autopilot.ts supplies the model-owned decision procedure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the supervisor model while leaving logs, cron and dispatch endpoints intact removes the judgment that selects actionable issues, deduplicates them semantically and chooses current worker allocation. - Evidence: README Autopilot; src/autopilot.ts; src/mcp.ts; supervisor routes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human review/merge remains downstream authority over code admission; S3 credit is for autonomous current investigation allocation/control, not autonomous deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no qualifying independent audit loop over an S1 claim was established inside the deployed Dodo boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers run focused tests and open draft PRs, and humans review those PRs; ordinary coding sessions may perform PR-review tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent auditor owns a complementary verdict over another Dodo producer and closes correction/re-review. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tests, Git diffs/PRs, human review requirement, optional review prompts. - Closure path: worker findings reach a draft PR/human; the human/repository-development path is external parent governance rather than first-party S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an operational agent can perform “review” as its assigned S1 task, but no distinct autonomous auditor relationship is wired into standard runtime delivery. - Evidence: README Autopilot; docs/session-goals.md; repository review statements. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human review is real assurance but cannot be published as S3 under this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: browser research, persistent memory and autopilot log investigation help current tasks/current failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path models future/external change, develops adaptation options and returns a selected option into present whole-system capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: browser/CDP tools, memory, skills, model routing and current-failure autopilot. - Closure path: external/current evidence can shape the active coding task, but no prospective strategic adaptation loop is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task web research and current-error repair are not the Profile&#39;s outside-and-then function. - Evidence: README Browser/Memory/Autopilot sections. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: self-repair can change future code after human merge, but its trigger/judgment is current failure remediation rather than external/prospective intelligence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: admin allowlists, sharing permissions, secrets, model config, goal rules and autopilot hard rules constrain operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level issue is routed to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as durable system-governing policy through a first-party closure loop. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SharedIndex/UserControl permissions, admin guards, configuration, static prompts and tool permissions. - Closure path: constraints apply directly to operations, but no S5 issue/authority/return cycle is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: security/permission settings and hard-coded supervisor rules bound autonomy without constituting identity governance. - Evidence: README architecture/security sections; MCP/admin surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deployment owners retain ultimate authority outside Dodo.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8f1f39d97229af1df0e18fa32b459ffef0e3e89b/assessments/dodo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8f1f39d97229af1df0e18fa32b459ffef0e3e89b\">8f1f39d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T17:44:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/revfactory/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Revfactory Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/revfactory/harness/tree/92d9f1b29e7af81acebf51a9fcd1832e739a1414\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 92d9f1b29e7af81acebf51a9fcd1832e739a1414\">92d9f1b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform goal-conditioned, first-party skill-directed construction and repair of a domain-specific agent harness&#39;s project files. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown project&#39;s domain/tasks, preexisting skill/agent inventory, changing work graph and coordination mode suitability, model/quality requirements, missing trigger/role artifacts and user-supplied constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The Claude Code agent executing the shipped harness skill decides among control-flow modes, project-specific expert roles, generated skills, schemas and orchestration layout in response to inspected inputs; it writes and checks those actual project files. - Decision owner: Skill-guided model-based operating actor in supported Claude Code invocation; the external Claude runtime supplies cognition and tools but first-party skill links those discretionary choices to construction outputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skill steps, templates, mode-selection reference, target .claude file paths, generated metadata/trigger, routine QA rules, user goal and installation mechanism. - Closure path: User requests project harness → agent inspects existing project files and relevant constraints → autonomously chooses generated team/mode/skill implementation → writes the agent/skill/orchestrator files → validates files/triggers and reports construction to user; follow-up can revise them. - Boundary reachability: docs/quickstart.md documents installation, claude &quot;build a harness ...&quot;, expected concrete project files and test route. The plugin&#39;s harness skill is the operative first-party source of those reachable decisions, not a standalone backend. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the skill-guided model and no dynamic domain-to-team architecture/skill configuration can be selected or written by the static Markdown/templates alone. Human-supplied project request scopes the task rather than making each design decision. - Evidence: docs/quickstart.md, skills/harness/SKILL.md, skills/harness/SKILL.md, skills/harness/SKILL.md, README.md. - Basis: Explicit first-party shipped skill work instructions with documented installed model/file-change entry and output files. - Confidence: Medium-high structural capability; no model inference or downstream actual project build executed during assessment. - Caveats: The model and file tools are from the user&#39;s installed Claude Code; the skill does not guarantee successful execution independent of that host or budget.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party builder-level interference-specific stabilization of two viable simultaneous operating S1 units with changed behavior feedback is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Generated target teams may later experience shared-file overwrite and role conflict; generated workflow mode may propose frozen handoff artifacts, but these are future target configurations, not actual competing S1 departments of the first-party constructor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The builder model chooses a future team architecture; the selected future workflow/persistent agents and shared tasks are not live first-party S2 regulation of builder-owned operating units. - Decision owner: Model for constructor task choice; deterministic/external Claude tools for future workflow execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pipeline, parallel, SendMessage, task lists, artifact-freeze instructions and adversarial review recipes provided as generated-configuration templates. - Closure path: Project-analysis requirements → model specifies future coordination configuration → writes target orchestrator file. The documented construction process does not run distinct competing builder S1 units, sense their actual mutual interference, choose an adjustment and feed a stabilized outcome into those units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constructor discretion is S1 design work here, not already exercised S2 regulation; the phrase &quot;agent team&quot; and syntactic SendMessage template are insufficient evidence of an inter-S1 regulator. - Evidence: skills/harness/references/execution-modes.md, skills/harness/references/execution-modes.md, skills/harness/SKILL.md, README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative at explicit constructor recursion after testing targeted generated-team paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Independently deployed generated target teams may have S2; they require their own separate source/runtime boundary and assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current manager holding discretionary live resource, priority and accountability decisions across multiple operating units of the constructor system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Constructor&#39;s task complexity, budget guidance, execution mode suitability and quality errors require local project build control; they are not ongoing contested commitments of a current organizational portfolio. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Builder model chooses current design/work plan and verification; external user/operator grants use of costly Workflow; no distinct S3 holds present portfolio-level authority across multiple viable operating builder cells. - Decision owner: Model for S1 project design, operator for permission/limits, host for deterministic tool constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Phases 0–7, cost/model tier heuristics, predefined orchestration mode, QA checks, tool permission constraints. - Closure path: Inadequate generated file or user restriction → model revises local build, permissions control chosen tool use, not whole-current executive negotiation and cross-unit operating-priority feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model controls current project artifacts as primary construction work, not a separate organizational inside-and-now executive function; a permission gate is enforcement. - Evidence: skills/harness/SKILL.md, skills/harness/SKILL.md, skills/harness/references/execution-modes.md, docs/quickstart.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after role/mode/approval and build quality verification inspection. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The surrounding human development team and a target generated team&#39;s eventual S3 are not within this tested first-party builder boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently sourced complementary reality audit of an S3 current executive&#39;s reports with distinct corrective judgment/return is wired into the constructor&#39;s own operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Generated-skill formatting, trigger mismatches, agent-role conflicts and QA omissions can be checked, but these are ordinary construction acceptance/repair duties rather than an independent assessment of an S3 claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The builder model runs mandatory QA and may generate a future critic or adversarial Workflow, but no independently mandated S3 auditor is shown with alternative evidence and corrective control over the current constructor organization. - Decision owner: Same builder/revision model and routine host validation, not a separate first-party audit authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QA reference, with/without-skill A/B test recommendations, producer-reviewer team patterns, completeness critics, skill frontmatter and trigger checks. - Closure path: Generated output → routine skill/QA inspection → ordinary correction of builder output; optional generated target critic is a future separate deployment, not a current independent audit feedback path to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: More than one proposed critic agent is an architecture choice, not proof that a distinct first-party complementary audit witness is actually run with independent operational access. - Evidence: README.md, skills/harness/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/harness/references/execution-modes.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting QA, verification, potential external agents and feedback. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: User-run independent audits or deployed generated multi-agent QA are adjacent systems and could change the analysis at another recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: A user reports unexpected quality/behavior, repeated correction requests, failed agent retries, missing skill triggers, or user overrides of the orchestrator; evolve also inspects Git and actual workspace/ execution artifacts to distinguish new operating reality from planned design. These are external user and operating-environment signals, not just internal chain-of-thought or stored prompts. - Future / prospective distinction: The next invocation needs a revised instruction/agent role/trigger/workflow to prevent recurrence; the skill compares the original harness with its observed current version and identifies pattern-level improvement rather than reacting once to the same run. - Adaptation option generated: The skill-guided model maps observations to candidate changes (skill quality rule, agent role, orchestrator sequence, trigger description, token-policy adjustment), generalizes the feedback, selects and edits one at a time with reason, or routes major redesign to harness; user confirms conflicting revisions. - Path back into current capability / S3: It first inspects current .claude agents, skills, CLAUDE.md change history and execution traces; then persists validated changes into those same current operational artifacts. The next project run reads the edited skills/orchestrator, closing two-way feedback from present operating configuration → future distinction → changed present capability. This is a functional inside-and-now capability interface, not evidence of a separately autonomous S3 owner. - Function: Reassess future suitability of existing user-project agent, skill and orchestration capabilities against changing user expectations and evidence of actual operating failures, choose generalizable improvements, and return them into subsequent harness execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing task requirements, recurring user correction patterns, agent output quality defects, failed workflow/trigger activation, resource inefficiency and v1-to-v2 compatibility drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Once evolve is invoked, the skill-guided model reads current harness Git/file state and external user feedback or observed execution traces, classifies the mismatch, develops a generalizable adaptation (skill rules, agent roles, orchestrator or trigger), chooses and edits the target files one change at a time; it validates and records the rationale for future use. This is beyond an internal task plan or a memory summary. - Decision owner: Model-backed harness:evolve actor for ordinary prospective adjustment within bounded authorizations; the user provides the trigger/feedback and is asked to approve contradictory reversals; major structural redesign is routed to harness, not silently taken by evolve. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git delta review, workspace trace inspection, feedback prompts, classification table, CLAUDE.md persistent history, trigger/frontmatter checks and one-at-a-time regression guard. - Closure path: Existing target operating configuration/history + actual user/execution feedback → source-guided option/generalization/choice → project-local agent/skill/orchestrator edit → validation and change history → next installed Claude Code invocation reads the changed files, altering its future working capacity. harness inventory/build and current project artifacts provide the current-capability side; evolve proposes and returns prospective modifications to that same operating configuration. - Boundary reachability: Separate bundled skills/evolve/SKILL.md is installed with the main plugin and documented &quot;evolve the harness with this feedback&quot; / &quot;하네스 회고해줘&quot; invocation in README/quickstart; Phase 3 explicitly requires applying agent/skill/orchestrator changes and Phase 4 validation/history. The closure is supported by first-party shipped mode, not a repository developer&#39;s proposed future feature. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model&#39;s interpretation/generalization, Git/log scanning and static Markdown cannot pick appropriate future changes nor write semantic refinements; operator feedback triggers and bounds but does not routinely decide each adaptation. Fixed validators enforce the chosen edit without owning future strategy. - Evidence: skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, docs/quickstart.md, README.md. - Basis: Explicit first-party mode with external/user-feedback sensing, future-capability option selection and artifact write/validation closure. - Confidence: Medium-high for structural user-invoked agent-owned future adaptation; no actual live evolve run was executed in this review. - Caveats: This is on-demand S4 (not continuous background environment scanning). Major redesign and contradictory revision require routing/parent consent, but they do not establish a separate first-party S4 parent-governed mode without a corresponding legitimate decision-and-return trace; published state is A, not A(P). No claim that every change measurably improves external task performance without empirical evaluation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate ultimate-purpose/organizational identity arbitration and binding policy return to the harness system is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User sets domain/goal and whether to incur large multi-agent cost; conflicting requested changes can require consent. These are project configuration and revision safety questions, not constitutional mission/identity decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/developer supplies the project&#39;s purpose and authorizes tool/Workflow use. Builder model structures the team and evolve proposes scoped improvements, but neither is granted power to determine enduring ultimate project policy or resolve genuine identity conflict through a legitimate parent governance channel. - Decision owner: External user/host for mission, permissions and ultimate legitimacy; model for subordinate design/adaptation decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static phase/mode constraints, approval/consent on conflicting revisions, generated CLAUDE.md trigger/history, host permissions and budget directives. - Closure path: User-specified task/permission → model design/edit under existing limits; no packaged identity-level matter intake → legitimate authority decision → changed enduring governing purpose through an S5 return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Being allowed to rewrite a skill in a user project is not the mandate to decide the team&#39;s institutional ultimate purpose; ordinary permission for Workflow does not create S5. - Evidence: skills/harness/SKILL.md, skills/harness/references/execution-modes.md, skills/evolve/SKILL.md, README.md. - Basis: Source-bounded negative after inspecting approval and policy-adjacent paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The host organization&#39;s real parent authority remains outside this first-party skill&#39;s own governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9845f69381cab2f28e5818c1738ae04957a00a56/assessments/re/revfactory-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9845f69381cab2f28e5818c1738ae04957a00a56\">9845f69 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T02:53:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cisco-ai-defense/defenseclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DefenseClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cisco-ai-defense/defenseclaw/tree/85029e57e9094debd9366a7d5a9f7e6d5c3ef6fc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85029e57e9094debd9366a7d5a9f7e6d5c3ef6fc\">85029e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: make contextual security judgments over protected agent traffic/actions and close those judgments into the execution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prompt injection, PII/secret exfiltration, unsafe or destructive tool activity, ambiguous tool-call chains, malicious capabilities/assets and benign cases that static matching alone cannot distinguish reliably. - Decisive decision or feedback right: classify the current protected content/action using the model-backed Judge and contribute a semantic verdict that policy can turn into allow, alert, confirm or block. - Decision owner: the first-party LLM Judge role in configured open-source/local Judge mode; deterministic scanners and policy constrain/merge its judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local pattern scanner; rule packs; OPA/fallback policy; asset policy; Cisco inspection when configured; connector hooks; audit persistence; fail modes. - Closure path: protected prompt/tool/result arrives → local evidence and optional Judge classification are produced → guardrail/policy finalizes the verdict → action-mode connector/hook returns an enforceable block/confirm/allow result → the protected operation proceeds or is stopped. - Boundary reachability: LLMJudge, GuardrailInspector, inspect endpoints and native connector enforcement are shipped runtime surfaces; the operator configures the model/provider but does not need to invent the safety role or execution feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the Judge model, deterministic and external lanes remain, but the first-party runtime loses its contextual semantic classification over ambiguous prompt/tool content. The model-backed role therefore owns real S1 discretion in that supported mode. - Evidence: internal/gateway/llmjudge.go; internal/gateway/guardrail.go; internal/gateway/inspect.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: protected-agent task reasoning is outside the boundary. Managed-enterprise mode can assign authoritative enforcement to the external Cisco AI Defense service; the positive local state is based on the shipped open-source Judge mode, not inherited cloud ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material same-recursion inter-S1 coordination function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple detector lanes and protected connectors exist, but detector aggregation is one safety-decision pipeline rather than coordination among distinct autonomous operational S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an interference-specific relation among distinct same-recursion S1 units. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verdict merging; per-connector policy; hook routing; asset-policy resolution; scanner-source arbitration. - Closure path: evidence from several lanes is merged into one safety verdict; no separate inter-S1 disturbance→coordination response→changed S1 behavior loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routing and arbitration among detector outputs are not S2 without distinct operational units and an evidenced mutual interference. - Evidence: internal/gateway/guardrail.go; internal/gateway/assetpolicyruntime.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a higher-level fleet/security platform may coordinate multiple agents, but that recursion is not DefenseClaw&#39;s local safety pipeline.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current protected session trajectory and intervene in the next tool action using a bounded whole-session view. - Disturbance / variety regulated: harmful multi-step tool chains, mismatch between current user intent and later tool activity, risk that emerges only across successive actions, and current security findings that should stop or escalate the next operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the next tool call with the current session&#39;s user intent and recent tool-call sequence, then feed the resulting security action into the execution hook. - Decision owner: the model-backed LLM Judge in the configured tool-injection/current-session mode, constrained by guardrail policy and deterministic evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: toolJudgeSessionContext; ObserveSessionPrompt; bounded recent-call history; RunToolJudge; guardrail merge/finalization; action-mode hook enforcement. - Closure path: authenticated session prompt records current intent → successive tool calls accumulate bounded session history → next call is judged against intent + prior calls + current arguments → policy emits block/confirm/allow → native hook changes the next current operation. - Boundary reachability: session context and Judge methods are wired into the production gateway/hook inspection path, and action-mode verdicts are consumed by shipped connector hooks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the current-session supervisory distinction comes from the model&#39;s interpretation of intent plus trajectory; deterministic machinery supplies state/enforcement but cannot reproduce the same contextual judgment after the model is removed. - Evidence: internal/gateway/llmjudge.go; internal/gateway/inspect.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the session context is deliberately bounded and in-memory; this is current safety control, not durable strategic adaptation. - Whole-system current view: latest bounded user intent plus up to the configured bounded recent same-session tool events and the proposed current tool call, combined with current detector/policy evidence. - Current-control decision scope: whether the next protected tool operation may proceed, should alert/require confirmation, or must be blocked now.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: durable audit, finding/correlation stores, scans and observability supply complementary evidence but do not establish a closed independent audit judgment returning directly into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: historical findings, runtime decisions, correlations, inventory and security evidence can expose conditions not visible from one current hook decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as a first-party complementary auditor whose finding itself enters a mandatory/authoritative current-control feedback path. - Decision owner: operator/external security workflow for follow-up; audit code primarily records, queries, correlates and exports evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: audit store; finding lifecycle; correlation; observability exporters; CLI audit/findings actions; scans; alerts. - Closure path: runtime/security evidence → durable audit/findings surfaces → operator/external workflow may change policy/configuration; the standard runtime does not show the independent audit finding itself closing the subsequent control transition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an extensive evidence plane is not sufficient for S3 when the return-to-control decision remains external. - Evidence: internal/audit/store.go; internal/audit/correlationv8.go; cli/defenseclaw/auditactions.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external SOC/managed workflows can close a complementary audit loop at a parent recursion; that ownership is not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing threat patterns, connector versions, policy/rule packs, registries and cloud inspection capabilities can affect future security posture. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for autonomously developing and selecting a future capability adaptation from external change. - Decision owner: operator/maintainer/configuration for rule-pack, policy, registry and connector changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: policy reload; local-pattern overrides; registry sync; connector compatibility data; scanner updates; managed remote inspection. - Closure path: external/operator updates policy/rules/configuration → DefenseClaw reloads/applies them → later runtime decisions change; no autonomous environment-model→adaptation-option→return loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hot reload and registry/policy synchronization implement chosen adaptations; they do not autonomously choose them from prospective environmental analysis. - Evidence: internal/gateway/guardrail.go; cli/defenseclaw/enforce/policy.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model-backed runtime classification adapts the current verdict to context but does not create durable future capability by itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: policy engines, allow/block registries, HILT settings and fail modes enforce ordinary security policy without identity/ultimate-policy closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: admissible assets, severity actions, runtime enforcement posture, confirmation requirements, scanner/fail modes and connector restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue at this recursion. - Decision owner: operator/administrator/configuration supplies the policy; DefenseClaw interprets and enforces it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OPA/Rego; policy bundles; asset policy; manual allow/block lists; HILT; fallback profiles; admission rules. - Closure path: externally selected security policy → first-party evaluation/enforcement → later protected operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: strong policy enforcement and final veto authority over ordinary actions do not themselves constitute S5 identity governance. - Evidence: internal/gateway/guardrail.go; cli/defenseclaw/enforce/admission.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: enterprise governance may place DefenseClaw inside an S5 process at a higher recursion; that parent authority is outside the assessed system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d2f259676420ef28f43c24248975238991e4879b/assessments/defenseclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d2f259676420ef28f43c24248975238991e4879b\">d2f2596 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T18:11:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kai-linux/agent-os\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent-OS (kai-linux)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kai-linux/agent-os/tree/d7330ebc5e117f06df1fdf23118af8da2c327507\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d7330ebc5e117f06df1fdf23118af8da2c327507\">d7330eb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Task-conditioned software engineering by independently operating model-driven coding workers launched, bounded and observed through first-party Agent-OS execution paths. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable task requirements, live source repository and environment state, tool errors, code-test failures and external model outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Worker model chooses specific coding actions/edits/tests in an isolated checkout and reports result; host runs it as a concrete accepted task. - Decision owner: Configured model-driven coding worker (Codex/OMP/Claude etc.) acting in the first-party launched Agent-OS execution envelope; model provider internals remain external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QueueEngine task selection, worker CLI adapter, isolated checkout, prompt/task contract, result parser, attempt budget and run log. - Closure path: Accepted GitHub task → model-guided coding in managed worktree → changed files and result artifact → PR/CI observation and correction/retry/merge feedback. - Boundary reachability: The shipped queue.py dispatches real external agent executables against its own per-task worktrees and parses their artifacts, rather than displaying a static plan (orchestrator/queue.py; orchestrator/qualifiedworker.py; README.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The worker performs open-ended task-specific decisions; deterministic Agent-OS delivery guards provide an operating envelope, not external provider authorship. - Evidence: orchestrator/queue.py; orchestrator/qualifiedworker.py; README.md - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent concurrent independent S1 coding workers from corrupting shared project/repository state and manage collisions during integration/PR activity. - Distinct S1 units: Independently launched coding agents operating on distinct task worktrees for the same managed repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent modifications or git/PR operations can contend over common repository integration state. - Attenuating coordination relation: Worktree separation, per-repository locks and explicit CI/conflict-aware PR integration rules. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Queued/blocked work, conflict/rebase failure and bounded retry/escalation alter the next worker attempt/commitment. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The relation directly protects shared editing/integration resources under actual competing S1 work rather than being only a generic backlog or message queue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two or more workers can edit branches/files for one repository, race over git state, or return incompatible work into the same integration target. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Enforce per-repository locking, isolate task checkout state and reject/route conflicting integration/PR states rather than allowing concurrent overwrites. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic supervisor/worktree and PR merge/rebase gate, with external Git providing transactional conflict detection. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task-specific git worktrees, worker isolation, per-repo locks, exclusive task processing and PR Monitor conflict/rebase policies. - Closure path: Competing work admissions → repo/worktree protection and merge conflict signals → serialize/rebase/retry/escalate → changed worker eligibility and next operating assignment. - Boundary reachability: The supported queue and supervisor actually create isolated task worktrees and enforce repo-specific locks; PR monitor processes live conflicts and retries/rebases under explicit rules (orchestrator/queue.py; orchestrator/supervisor.py; orchestrator/prmonitor.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Conflicts are between independently acting code workers, and the attenuation/feedback path is a real running coordination mechanism but deterministically owned (C). - Evidence: orchestrator/queue.py; orchestrator/workerisolation.py; orchestrator/supervisor.py; orchestrator/prmonitor.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Regulate current delivery obligations, worker assignment, attempt budgets, PR waiting/merge status and run-level interventions across a managed project/program. - Whole-system current view: Accepted goal/project/program and worker/PR lifecycle state with retries, active leases, constraints and waiting steps. - Current-control decision scope: Live worker allocation, current priority, attempt ceilings, PR acceptance, stall recovery and escalations within a managed repository/program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open goals, unassigned backlog, agent failures/quota limits, stalled PRs, CI status, changing execution resources and disputed completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose admitted/pending work by queue priority and availability, select a worker fallback chain, retry within bounded policy, escalate irrecoverable failures and merge only after required current gates. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic Queue Engine / PR Monitor / delivery controller using accepted human goals and policy; LLM prompt formatting does not itself own whole-current control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent goal/queue state, task-priority labels, attempts and worker fallbacks, stage/status files, CI gate, PR monitor, webhook/GitHub feedback and human handoff. - Closure path: Current issue/goal and queue state → prioritized worker task/PR gate action → status/CI/result observations → retry, merge, blocker or escalation → next current delivery commitments. - Boundary reachability: The deployed service loop regularly runs queue dispatch and PR monitor and routes real GitHub and worker results back into retained goal/issue state (orchestrator/queue.py; orchestrator/prmonitor.py; orchestrator/deliveryprogram.py; docs/delivery.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive ongoing admission/priority/merge policy is implemented by deterministic service components; tools and model-generated issue descriptions support but do not own those decisions. - Evidence: orchestrator/queue.py; orchestrator/prmonitor.py; orchestrator/deliveryprogram.py; orchestrator/deliverycontract.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separately assess substantive PR code against the linked issue&#39;s acceptance criteria using primary diff evidence and an LLM reviewer from a model family independent of the coding worker. - Claim being audited: A worker&#39;s PR actually implements the linked accepted goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker .agentresult.md, PR body and own tests. - Complementary access path: WorkVerifier reads GitHub issue acceptance criteria and actual proposed source diff independently from worker report. - Independence boundary: Reviewer picks a different model family from the worker and is not the same coding model continuation. - Who acts on findings: PR Monitor blocks auto-merge and posts a block finding; worker retries or human override/repair returns to subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect/stubbed/incomplete code from an otherwise green or plausible worker result, acceptance mismatch or missing tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect first-party fetched PR diff and issue source, judge individual acceptance criteria/scope, block auto-merge and issue review findings or escalations. - Decision owner: Distinct first-party WorkVerifier model judgment, with independent model-family selection; deterministic PR Monitor enforces its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Raw GitHub issue and diff acquisition, code anti-pattern heuristics, fixed review rubric, distinct Claude/Gemini model family, report/override audit, PR Monitor merge block. - Closure path: Produced PR diff + independent issue criteria → independent model review → stored block/pass with specific findings → PR monitor blocks or permits merge and returns issue comment/Telegram signal for repair/override. - Boundary reachability: The PR monitor&#39;s live merge gate invokes verifypullrequest for each qualifying autonomous PR; WorkVerifier enforces a model-family difference from the producing agent and uses actual diff/issue source (orchestrator/workverifier.py; orchestrator/prmonitor.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate model makes the complementary artifact-judgment decision on independently sourced PR data; CI status itself, pass/fail parser and human approval are only enforcement. - Evidence: orchestrator/workverifier.py; orchestrator/prmonitor.py; docs/delivery.md - Basis: explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Future delivery adaptation through objective/outcome measurement, external research and model-proposed sprint planning, with legitimate human approval before changing operational commitments. - External distinction: Observes business/outcome criteria, external research and repository activity rather than just the current executing worker&#39;s tool output. - Future / prospective distinction: Forms next-sprint options and improvements after retrospectives, as opposed to merely selecting the next in-flight task. - Adaptation option generated: Model-created ranked future backlog/sprint plan constrained by approved goals and measured outcomes. - Path back into current capability / S3: Human approves selected plan; accepted tickets are promoted into ready work and executed by existing current-control machinery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future product/business objective gaps, external repository changes, recurring reliability risks and emerging capabilities/opportunities beyond a current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to accept/reject model-proposed future sprint priorities or capability work and thereby change ready backlog/current team commitments. - Decision owner: Human parent/operator for plan promotion; first-party strategic planner/model and research system propose but do not independently enact approved priority changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Objective/outcome scorecards, research context, log analysis, strategic model prompt, approval/expiry action, accepted plan promotion and queued tickets. - Closure path: Observed outcomes/external context → future sprint options proposed by model → explicit human approval/skip → accepted tasks promoted to Ready queue → current S3 dispatcher operates those commitments. - Boundary reachability: The shipped strategicplanner gathers objective/production/research inputs, model-produces a plan and refuses to apply it absent Telegram approval; approval triggers applyplanpromotions and subsequent queue execution (orchestrator/strategicplanner.py; orchestrator/objectives.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive adaptation commitment is parent-owned; model research/proposals do not constitute autonomous promotion. This is P, not simply scheduled recurrence or S3 task ordering. - Evidence: orchestrator/strategicplanner.py; orchestrator/objectives.py; orchestrator/agentscorer.py; orchestrator/backloggroomer.py - Basis: explicit + structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Human-chartered goals and autonomy tiers express purpose and authorization, but an operational ultimate-purpose policy revision cycle and complete returned governance feedback remain unestablished. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether ultimate organization purpose/charter can be revised as a governed feedback response. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human owner of accepted goal scope and rollout tiers, without demonstrated full systemic S5 loop. - Return-to-operation path: Specific human handoffs may update one accepted goal, but feedback into overarching governing policy is unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Legitimate changes of purpose/scope, policy risks, trust in automatic merge, unacceptable commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator sets original accepted-goal scope and tiers and handles escalations; no verified full ultimate-policy adaptation decision loop beyond accepted-delivery governance. - Decision owner: Human parent/operator and deterministic contract guards, but S5 functional closure remains unresolved at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal charters, targeted operating model, config scopes, autonomous-rollout stages, human approvals and override log. - Closure path: Policy/goal settings constrain work; escalation returns human decisions, but ultimate organizational identity/purpose review and returned generalized policy update are not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Final approval and bounded user goals do not automatically establish complete S5 governance. - Evidence: orchestrator/deliverycontract.py; orchestrator/deliveryprogram.py; targetoperatingmodel.yaml; orchestrator/repomodes.py; docs/delivery.md - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4a4bb5a54f7a1022bda445650e1e45b1a6781baa/assessments/kai-linux-agent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4a4bb5a54f7a1022bda445650e1e45b1a6781baa\">4a4bb5a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T15:32:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theo-kirby/chassis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chassis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theo-kirby/chassis/tree/b9350031580d58328849184974e1368d082f4892\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b9350031580d58328849184974e1368d082f4892\">b935003 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute model-led tasks such as research, coding, reporting and tool-based work in scheduled, triggered or interactive runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable task inputs, changing external research observations, file/tool outputs and success/failure results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each configured model agent chooses task-specific reasoning, tool calls and its returned report/verdict while running its own SYSTEM/INSTRUCTIONS context. - Decision owner: Pi or supported configured coding-agent/LLM runtime operating as the Chassis task agent; Chassis supplies accessible prompts and validated tools rather than precomputing the task response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run-agent, Pi extension, cron launcher, trigger router, tool dispatcher, audited tool results and isolated optional supervised jobs. - Closure path: task instruction/cron or earlier trigger → model agent run → inspected data/tools and outcome judgment → returned result/verdict and optional follow-on triggered tasks. - Boundary reachability: first-party Docker image seeds an agent runtime and driver; chassis run, cron and triggers are documented first-party execution modes, while the optional supervised job path is separately documented and implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a scheduler and schema-checked dispatcher can run or reject tools, but cannot choose the substantive task analysis or output, which remains with the configured LLM agent. - Evidence: README agent/task architecture and runtime, agent launch and feedback triggers, driver starter. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: credentials/model endpoint and individual task definitions must be provisioned; distributed examples do not include private notebook pilot configurations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no independently established agent-owned coordination regulator of disturbances among multiple live S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model task outputs may depend on one another, and badly configured trigger cycles can cause runaway repeated jobs; existing routing remains user-configured deterministic conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped autonomous coordinator judging current cross-agent interference and returning a corrective relation between still-operating units; trigger predicates merely apply preauthored conditions to prior verdict values. - Decision owner: operator-provided trigger graph plus run-agent parser/dispatcher; model agents own their individual verdict content, not the inter-unit coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task cron/trigger definitions, after/onsuccess/onfailure conditions and verdict K/V comparisons, detached setsid launch, optional single-worker supervisor limit. - Closure path: agent completion/verdict → configured predicate → following task is launched or skipped; generic event feedback does not by itself demonstrate S2 regulation of an inter-S1 interference/oscillation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the README&#39;s hypothetical writer/reviewer cycle is a user-composed workflow example; the shipped system does not define an S2-specific autonomous decision right over fleet interactions. - Evidence: README triggers and illustrative writer/reviewer flow, trigger evaluator, supervisor one-slot policy. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an operator can design a specific conflict-regulating multi-agent workflow using triggers, but generic condition-based event routing alone is insufficient for published C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: current whole-fleet supervision, revision of agent/task commitments and intervention in which operational agents and tools may act. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent outputs or run failures may show that instructions, tool availability, scheduled work or current fleet priorities no longer satisfy the local operator&#39;s goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in the agent-owned Conductor-like driver mode, the seeded model examines whole-fleet logs and definitions, chooses which agents/tasks need changes, and writes new prompts/configuration that subsequent model runs use; in the legitimate parent mode, the human independently chooses current runs, agent installation/definitions and protected tool/cron changes through host controls. - Decision owner: autonomous seeded driver AI when interactively invoked for its bounded editable agent-definition surface; the host operator in the distinct direct CLI/control mode. Root-enforced tool and cron restrictions remain constraints in both modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared /home/agent registry, per-agent run logs, dispatcher audit, agent definition files, chassis run/kill/runs/install-agent/reload-cron, dashboard and root tool gate. - Closure path: observed current fleet results/operating constraints → driver or human judges necessary agent/task changes → definitions, run state or refreshed cron/tools are changed through their respective permitted paths → subsequent task agents launch with updated commitments. - Boundary reachability: the official Docker image ships harness/agent-template/driver/SYSTEM.md and supports chassis run driver; the host chassis CLI and dashboard are part of the same published standard mode. Neither decision right is borrowed from source maintainer governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the driver model chooses material changes to other agents&#39; roles/tasks after inspecting the fleet, while deterministic cron/parser and secret protection cannot make this judgment; the human mode retains different, direct parent authority. - Whole-system current view: all local /home/agent definitions, aggregate agent run summaries, cron and dispatcher audit; host control additionally sees current containers, active/detached runs, dashboard status and protected configuration. - Current-control decision scope: fleet agent/task definitions, model choices and instructions under driver write scope; parent controls concrete run/stop, tooling, protected secrets, cron refresh and installation. - Evidence: seeded driver decisive rights and limits, main execution/CLI controls, README driver/fleet. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: driver is interactive/operator-invoked, not an always-on self-starting fleet overseer; model edits to cron require separate operator reload-cron. The A(P) describes alternative supported ownership closures, not simultaneous ultimate authorities.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no bundled agent-owned independent audit of fleet control or operations with complementary access and returned corrective decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bad reports or a mistaken driver configuration could need independent checking, but the ordinary fleet does not ship an autonomous auditor that can judge those claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established beyond the original agent&#39;s verdicts, deterministic schema/exit checks and optional human review. - Decision owner: task agents emit verdicts; operator reads dashboards/reports; supervisor validates structure, not semantic truth. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dispatch audit, trigger logs, model per-run records, structured supervised job reports, SQLite history and host Gmail summaries. - Closure path: run outputs and logs → optional human inspection or schema checks; no first-party independent reviewer agent judging raw evidence and returning corrections to S3 is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user can configure a separate reviewer in the generic trigger graph, but README&#39;s illustration does not ship the reviewer actor, independent evidence access and feedback authority as a standard-deployment mode. - Evidence: README example review flow, supervisor report validation caveat, runtime log/trigger model. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this does not claim that custom user-authored reviewer agents are impossible; they are outside a bundled independent audit route.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate prospective environmental sensing, adaptation option and adoption loop established for Chassis&#39;s future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external changes and the future operating environment may require new agent behaviors, but the driver is instructed to repair observed current fleet performance, not to conduct autonomous future-environment intelligence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no dedicated first-party future-oriented external analysis/adoption mandate established. - Decision owner: human defines future task lineup, external tool/secret expansions and maintenance; driver handles operator-invoked observed current agent-definition adjustments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: editable agent prompts/models, scheduled tasks, public web tools, user configuration and optional notebook watch jobs. - Closure path: current operation feedback → possible present driver correction; no separately evidenced outside/future distinction → prospective adaptation option → changed capability feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the ability to edit task prompts or research the web if asked is not proof of a shipped S4 agent with future-environment responsibility. - Evidence: driver task/permission boundary, supervisor development status and next steps. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a particular deployed research fleet could form an S4 loop; the bundled framework and paused job examples do not establish that standing function at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate-purpose/identity policy adjudication for the local fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: secret leakage, permission boundaries, unauthorized tool calls and operator instructions need enforcement but do not by themselves produce a genuine identity-level dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent/parent S5 matter with authoritative policy decision returned to the whole fleet; the host operator sets static permissions and operating configuration. - Decision owner: parent human maintains tool/secret control outside the agent-user runtime, while privileged dispatcher enforces declared rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: two-user filesystem, root-owned .env, strict tools JSON schema, sudo allowlist, root-only supervised policy and audits. - Closure path: permission/tool policy → dispatcher validation or refusal → task result/audit; no identity-level issue escalates to legitimate ultimate authority for a new binding policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety boundaries and static purpose in SYSTEM.md constrain agents rather than endowing one with ultimate-policy adjudication rights. - Evidence: README security and scope, dispatcher implementation, supervised allowlist enforcement. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the user can change tool/secret policy on the host, but that is a protected operational configuration action rather than a reconstructed S5 identity-policy loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f5e80eb13dae8219e799d736cb8ac7588689245a/assessments/ch/chassis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f5e80eb13dae8219e799d736cb8ac7588689245a\">f5e80eb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"4\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T20:12:57Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentwasp/agentwasp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WASP</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>4</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentwasp/agentwasp/tree/8282a76f57aa4d0b7f2071321f064b26be942024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8282a76f57aa4d0b7f2071321f064b26be942024\">8282a76 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute operator objectives through an iterative model/tool loop and bounded goal runtime that can absorb tool/environment results and choose subsequent operational action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request ambiguity, tool/API/file results, failed skill calls, changing goal/task state, external data and intermediate observations that alter the next useful action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose model-driven skill calls and subsequent action/recovery across the LLM loop, and for autonomous goals generate and execute task plans within the configured repertoire. - Decision owner: the model-driven WASP operational agent/goal actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: intent fast paths, Context Builder, SkillExecutor, model manager, GoalOrchestrator, deterministic validators/guards, budgets, persistence, tool adapters and retry limits. - Closure path: operator/autonomous objective enters the runtime → model chooses skills/actions → first-party executor runs them → observations/errors return to model/goal state → model continues, replans where applicable or completes → result returns to the operator/environment. - Boundary reachability: the model/skill loop and Goal Engine are first-party standard operating surfaces documented and wired by the shipped agent-core runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic routing, limits and post-response guards constrain the loop, but substantive ordinary skill/action choices and goal planning are made by model inference rather than by those enforcement mechanisms. - Evidence: execution-pipeline.md; goalorchestrator/orchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: fast paths and final validators can deterministically handle or constrain some requests. The A claim is based on the standard model-driven execution/goal path, not on every possible fast path being agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among multiple persistent autonomous agents that share finite model, token, CPU and execution capacity in one WASP deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneously RUNNING agents can collectively monopolize model/provider capacity, exceed the global token budget, overload CPU or create excessive concurrent steps, degrading other S1 units despite each unit being locally valid. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise the cross-agent concurrency, shared-token and load-throttling regime that determines when an S1 may take its next step under shared pressure. - Decision owner: no autonomous standard-mode S2 owner is packaged. AgentOrchestrator deterministically enforces configured limits; the model-driven MetaSupervisor that could own richer team coordination is disabled by default and explicitly described as research-only at this revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: asyncio.Semaphore across agent steps, global Redis token counter, CPU threshold, active-agent store, shared model manager and deterministic throttling events. - Closure path: multiple S1 agents request/take ticks → shared concurrency/token/CPU conditions are evaluated → over-capacity work is deferred/throttled or serialized → capacity/next window becomes available → affected agents resume later. - Boundary reachability: shared throttles are wired directly into the standard AgentOrchestrator.tick() path used by persistent agents; no adopter-defined transport is needed for the coordination relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the inter-S1 disturbance and attenuation path are explicit, but the decisive regime is static/configured and mechanically enforced. An autonomous coordination actor would have to be composed/enabled beyond the credited standard mode, so the state is C, not A. - Evidence: agentmanager/orchestrator.py; agent-orchestration.md; config.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Plan Critic is not credited as S2 because validating one goal plan does not regulate interference among distinct S1 units. The disabled MetaSupervisor corroborates a richer possible pattern but does not upgrade the credited runtime mode. - Distinct S1 units: two or more persistent autonomous AgentRuntime instances with separate objectives, goal queues and execution contexts hosted by the same AgentOrchestrator. - Inter-S1 disturbance: these agents share model infrastructure, CPU and global token/step capacity, so aggregate activity can starve or overload peers even when no individual agent violates its own goal semantics. - Attenuating coordination relation: the orchestrator serializes/bounds simultaneous agent steps, stops all new agent ticks when the global token budget is exhausted and skips ticks under CPU overload. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: throttled agents do not take the current step; once the semaphore/window/load permits, a later tick re-enters their runtime and their next operational action occurs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited mechanism exists specifically to regulate contention created by multiple autonomous agents sharing finite resources, not merely to pass messages or assign subtasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: maintain an organization-wide current view of operational pressure/commitments and intervene in current autonomous activity through aggregate throttling or parent supervisory controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excessive active-goal pressure, recent operational error rate, high latency, runaway memory growth, CPU saturation, resource-cap exhaustion and current agent commitments that should be paused/resumed on behalf of the whole deployment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which current organizational activity is allowed to proceed under aggregate pressure and, in the parent mode, decide which persistent agents remain active, paused or resumed after reviewing current organization state. - Decision owner: base C mode — deterministic CPI/Resource Governor/AgentOrchestrator rules execute configured current-control responses with no autonomous S3 owner. Parent P mode — the single self-hosting operator owns supervisory pause/resume/archive decisions through the first-party all-agent dashboard. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CPI component collectors and agent:cpihigh; Resource Governor counters/caps; AgentOrchestrator status persistence; dashboard all-agent snapshot showing status, active goals and cognitive budgets; scheduler jobs that honor CPI gating. - Closure path: current whole-system pressure/status is gathered → base deterministic controls gate background/current work, or the parent operator reviews the all-agent snapshot and chooses pause/resume/archive → AgentOrchestrator persists the new status/removes or recreates runtime state → subsequent ticks follow the changed current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: CPI/governor controls are wired into standard scheduler/runtime operation, while the all-agent dashboard and pause/resume/archive endpoints are shipped first-party operator surfaces over the same AgentOrchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: WASP has concrete whole-system current-control structure, but the base current-control judgments are configured thresholds rather than model-owned discretion. The operator dashboard supplies a separately closed parent supervisory mode, hence C(P) rather than A. - Evidence: agent/cpi.py; scheduler/cpimonitor.py; resource-governor.md; dashboard/routes/agents.py; agentmanager/orchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: per-goal replanning is local S1/current-task recovery, not whole-system S3 by itself. Parent credit relies on the all-agent status/budget view plus operative pause/resume closure, not merely on a generic human edit or stop button. - Whole-system current view: CPI combines active-goal count, recent error rate, latency, memory growth and CPU; the dashboard separately lists every persistent agent with current status, active-goal count/IDs and cognitive-budget usage. - Current-control decision scope: base rules gate background autonomous/perception/dream work and enforce shared operational caps; the parent can remove an agent from active operation by pausing/archiving it or restore it with resume, changing current commitments represented in subsequent ticks.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge WASP&#39;s ordinary self-model and epistemic claims with materially different direct operational evidence and return discrepancies into subsequent control/state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the agent can believe it has strengths or domain confidence that no longer match actual observed skill outcomes, while silent cognitive subsystems or recent error spikes can remain invisible in the self-model&#39;s ordinary narrative. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether claimed strengths/confidence are contradicted by direct skill success rates, audit-log outcomes or missing cognitive heartbeats and decide which self-model/epistemic corrections follow. - Decision owner: the standard distribution supplies the full complementary evidence and corrective path, but the decisive audit judgment is encoded in deterministic thresholds/rules in SelfIntegrityMonitorJob; no autonomous auditor owns that judgment in the credited mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: direct SQL reads from auditlog and cognitive-system tables, Redis self-model/epistemic state, success-rate thresholds, drift threshold, report persistence, automatic self-model removal/epistemic correction and improvement queueing. - Closure path: ordinary self-model/epistemic claims persist → scheduled monitor independently reads raw operational success/error/heartbeat evidence → threshold comparisons produce findings → unsupported strengths are removed, epistemic confidence is corrected and improvement work can be queued → later reasoning uses the corrected state. - Boundary reachability: SelfIntegrityMonitorJob is imported and registered by the standard runtime and the default configuration exposes integritymonitorenabled=true; its evidence and correction stores are the same first-party runtime stores used by live cognition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the complementary access and feedback closure are real, but the audit judgment is deterministic rather than an autonomous agent&#39;s discretionary independent assessment. That is a function-specific constructor path (C), not A. - Evidence: scheduler/integrity.py; configuration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Plan Critic and final ResponseValidator are not used as the positive S3 witness because they are routine checks inside the production path rather than complementary access to operational reality. - Claim being audited: self-model assertions of strengths and epistemic confidence, plus the implicit claim that core cognitive producers are operating normally. - Ordinary reporting path: agent:selfmodel, agent:epistemic and ordinary runtime/cognitive state describe what WASP believes about its capability and confidence. - Complementary access path: SelfIntegrityMonitorJob reads measured skill success/failure data, recent auditlog anomalies and direct DB/Redis heartbeat evidence from the cognitive producers rather than relying on those self-claims. - Independence boundary: the scheduled monitor is separate from the model process that creates/uses the self-model and checks alternative operational records; however its verdict logic remains deterministic and therefore does not establish autonomous audit ownership. - Who acts on findings: first-party monitor actuators remove invalid strengths, overwrite drifted epistemic confidence and queue improvement items; the corrected state then affects subsequent cognition/operation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert explicit environmental/user correction signals into prospective behavioral adaptation that changes how future WASP operation reasons and acts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user corrections reveal that current behavior is systematically wrong — for example hallucination, inappropriate refusal, wrong-skill choice or missing-context handling — and repetition of that behavior would degrade future viability with the operator/environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret the correction context, formulate the durable future-facing rule/few-shot/anti-pattern that should prevent recurrence and admit that adaptation into active behavioral memory. - Decision owner: the model-driven BehavioralLearnerJob owns semantic rule extraction; deterministic safety filtering/deduplication/storage constrain and persist the result. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: correction-pattern detection, Redis pending queue, two-minute scheduler, adversarial-rule filter, behavioral-rules DB, dedup/conflict checks, future prompt injection and notification surfaces. - Closure path: user/environment correction is detected → exchange is queued → scheduled LLM analyzes the failure and generates a behavioral adaptation rule → safe active rule is persisted → Context Builder injects active learned rules/few-shots into future system prompts → subsequent S1 behavior operates under the adaptation. - Boundary reachability: Behavioral Learning is documented as default-enabled, registered in main.py, and its stored active rules are consumed by the standard prompt builder; no downstream learner or manual prompt edit is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic choice of what future behavior should change is produced by an autonomous model call. The queue, filters and database only transport/constrain that adaptation judgment, so the decisive S4 option-generation right is agent-owned. - Evidence: behavioral-learning.md; scheduler/behaviorallearner.py; memory/behavioral.py; configuration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic memory, current-goal replanning and Skill Evolution are not needed for this mapping. The Opportunity Engine is also not credited as a parent S4 mode because, at the pinned ref, its dashboard accepted endpoint only changes status; the documented action-execution closure is not present in the inspected route. - External distinction: a user correction is a first-party captured environmental distinction about how the running organization failed to meet operator expectations; the learner retains the request, bad response and explicit correction context. - Future / prospective distinction: the LLM is asked what rule the agent should follow to avoid the error in future interactions, turning the observed correction into a forward-looking behavioral distinction rather than merely recording the past event. - Adaptation option generated: a typed behavioral rule plus optional skillpoison and dynamic few-shot pair describing the changed future response/skill policy. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted-safe rules are saved active and injected into every later system prompt, directly modifying the operating repertoire used by subsequent S1 decisions; operator/dashboard surfaces can deactivate bad rules if needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: C(P) - Function: maintain and revise the WASP organization&#39;s explicit identity, purpose/autonomy posture and ultimate operating principles, with a dedicated constructor path and a separately closed single-operator parent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes or disputes about what the agent is for, how autonomous it should be, confirmation/risk posture, proactive behavior and other identity-level directives that should govern subsequent operation rather than one ordinary task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: authoritatively choose the current identity prompt (or restore a prior/default identity) that defines purpose/autonomy/policy posture for future model operation. - Decision owner: base C mode — IdentityManager exposes an identity-specific save/version/rollback/injection path but no autonomous ultimate-policy actor is wired to own it. Parent P mode — the single deployment operator owns the authoritative identity decision through the first-party dashboard. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Redis identity prompt/compiled state, version history, prompt compilation of autonomy/confirmation/risk/proactive directives, dashboard save/reset/rollback API, audit logging and Context Builder identity injection. - Closure path: identity/policy issue reaches the identity constructor or operator dashboard → a new/default/prior identity is selected → IdentityManager saves/compiles/version-controls it → subsequent prompt construction injects the active identity/directives → later S1 behavior is governed by the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: IdentityManager is initialized by the standard runtime and passed into prompt construction; dashboard identity routes call its live save/reset/rollback methods. Both constructor and parent paths are first-party surfaces of the assessed self-hosted deployment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: WASP intentionally supplies a function-specific identity decision/return primitive, supporting C, but does not wire an autonomous agent as legitimate ultimate identity authority. The standard closed authority is the deployment&#39;s single operator, supporting (P) rather than A. - Evidence: identity/manager.py; dashboard/routes/identity.py; agent/context.py; configuration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: prime.md, safety guards and ordinary task approvals are not independently treated as S5. Parent credit rests specifically on the Identity Engine&#39;s identity-level authoring/version/return path. Self-improvement of implementation code is not assumed to grant autonomous ultimate-policy authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the DEFAULTPROMPT explicitly states the organization&#39;s identity/purpose, autonomous operating mode and governing principles; IdentityManager compiles materially identity-level properties such as autonomy, confirmation threshold, risk tolerance and proactivity. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base C exposes the identity-specific decisive path but leaves legitimate autonomous authority to a downstream composition; parent P assigns authoritative selection to the self-hosting single operator through the dashboard identity editor/reset/rollback controls. - Return-to-operation path: operator/constructor decision → IdentityManager.save/reset/rollback → active prompt + compiled identity cache/Redis state → formatforprompt/Context Builder injection → subsequent model decisions operate under the selected identity.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c62efc8a5ab1194f3af914371786771519632c81/assessments/wasp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c62efc8a5ab1194f3af914371786771519632c81\">c62efc8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T22:01:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentenv/agentflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentFlow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentenv/agentflow/tree/eb51a2e7a5c7d65da5718c4a41843098b60c80e0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eb51a2e7a5c7d65da5718c4a41843098b60c80e0\">eb51a2e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select and execute novel task-specific coding/research actions within a graph node, using actual workspace/provider/tool feedback and returning an outcome to downstream subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different prompts and code/workspace states, external tool output and failures, dependency context, sequential retry inputs, varying model response and workflow outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the documented composed codex/claude/kimi/pi node mode, the configured model-backed CLI agent decides task-specific tools/actions and answer based on the rendered prompt and actual operating observations; first-party AgentFlow chooses node/runner and transports results, not the model&#39;s internal next action. - Decision owner: Configured model agent via the first-party NodeSpec → AgentAdapter → Runner integration; underlying CLI/provider cognition is external, but the shipped operation actually reaches it from the public Graph and returns task feedback into the same pipeline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG dependency resolver, agent adapter command builder, scoped provider and tool/sandbox options, local/Docker/SSH/EC2/ECS target, retry/failure graph, trace parser and persisted node results. - Closure path: User task graph → ready node prompt with upstream observations → prepared model-backed CLI execution → model-selected actions/tools until CLI result → captured output and success/trace → next dependent node or configured retry/review loop; completed task graph has user-facing output. - Boundary reachability: Public Graph DSL / agentflow run includes documented codex(), claude() and pi() nodes; Orchestrator.executenode resolves and prepares their first-party adapter and runner, and records results used by downstream graph nodes. The substantive model decisions occur within the explicitly external CLI runtime as a supported first-party composed mode. No general claim about a standalone AgentFlow-native LLM inner tool loop is made. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the configured model agent and graph scheduler still orders static commands but cannot produce task-specific reasoning/tool actions under unknown input. The first-party orchestration contributes a meaningful operational feedback envelope yet does not replace the external agent&#39;s cognition or own that provider&#39;s governance. - Evidence: README.md#L22, README.md#L45, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L962, agentflow/agents/codex.py#L120, agentflow/agents/pi.py#L17, agentflow/agents/registry.py#L1, agentflow/runners/local.py#L17. - Basis: Source-connected, documented model-backed CLI task path; this is not a verified live provider task completion. - Confidence: Medium-high for first-party integration to external model-backed action loop; remote provider and executable availability untested. - Caveats: Shell-only nodes can be deterministic; they are not the basis of this positive. A user-supplied graph&#39;s domain or a separately owned Codex multi-agent metasystem cannot be borrowed as AgentFlow&#39;s organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No source-backed distinct regulator of mutual disturbances between independently viable S1 operating divisions with binding coordinated adjustment to their own ongoing obligations is evidenced in this task-graph operating boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel tasks may write overlapping code, fanout outputs may disagree, node dependencies may race, and periodic monitors may cancel/rerun failing nodes; these are coordination problems inside one operator-authored graph rather than demonstrated interference between independent sustainable operational departments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller defines DAG dependency/fanout structure, success conditions and periodic watcher; scheduler applies predeclared readiness, while optional periodic model chooses cancel/rerun on watched current graph nodes. No separate model/program identifies actual inter-S1 departmental interference and adjusts commitments of two viable units. - Decision owner: Operator-authored graph and per-task actor/periodic monitor model, plus deterministic orchestration engine, not evidenced organizational S2 regulation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG/fanout/merge, success criteria, per-node retries, onfailure return edges, periodic action schema and cancellation/runner limits. - Closure path: Task outputs and run logs → existing graph or watcher issues scoped reruns/cancellations → same graph continues; no concrete inter-S1 independent-operation conflict → distinct S2 damping judgment → revised mutually coordinating operating rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model choosing task reruns is a current program-level tactic; it does not acquire a separate viable-system coordination role by serving many temporary agent nodes. - Evidence: agentflow/orchestrator.py#L844, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L890, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L1258, agentflow/dsl.py#L1, README.md#L47. - Basis: Bounded negative after examining both static DAG and live periodic action modes. - Confidence: Medium-high for defined graph-task recursion. - Caveats: A downstream application could compose an enduring multi-service organization with a genuinely empowered conflict regulator; not imported from generic graph semantics.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous whole-current portfolio executive that allocates discretionary present resources/priorities across independently viable S1 departments and returns binding adjustments is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Node overload, failure, delays, inference service availability and run cancellation can threaten a pipeline. Execution backpressure and periodic reruns manage this particular workflow rather than current enterprise-level viable-unit obligations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator chooses graph concurrency, maxiterations, failfast, tool grants, targets and cost exposure. Scheduler regulates active node queues; optional periodic model can command scoped cancel/rerun of watched nodes, not govern durable organization-level S1 budgets and obligations. - Decision owner: Human/application config and deterministic first-party scheduler; periodic actor model for scoped run intervention, not demonstrated whole-current S3 managerial authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RunStore, semaphores, cancellation/rerun flags, dependency tracking, sandbox/credential controls, graph state machine, trace events, periodic action handling. - Closure path: Node progress/errors → ready set/failure gate or optional periodic action → specific graph node rerun/cancel → current run continues/terminates; absent executive assessment/decision/return across a portfolio of independently viable operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong task-program scheduling does not automatically possess a separate enterprise-management mandate over multiple S1 divisions. - Evidence: agentflow/orchestrator.py#L94, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L609, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L890, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L1258, agentflow/specs.py#L2332. - Basis: Source-bounded negative including optional model-backed periodic current-control behavior. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A higher application may delegate S3 decision rights via additional policies not shipped by AgentFlow at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent audit of organizational S1/S3 operational reality that sends corrective evidence to an actual S3 management owner and alters whole-current control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong outputs, failed tests, incomplete node logs, missing artifacts and failed optimizer loading can undermine conclusions. Model review steps and success criteria give task-level acceptance/retry evidence but do not form a separately mandated audit of management claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluatesuccess applies configured criteria to node output/artifacts, reviewers act as task-specific agent nodes, trace and graph reports store what happened. No separate first-party independent checker decides that an S3 managerial picture is wrong and mandates a corrective cross-operation response. - Decision owner: User-configured success validators and task model reviewer, immutable run storage for data, not distinct operational reality auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NodeAttempt/NodeResult receipts, trace parsing, success criteria, graphoptimizer GRAPHREPORT evidence, validation/retry path and node report. - Closure path: Task/node output → validator/reviewer/trace → task-level acceptance or graph retry; no independent complementary S3 inspection → managerial reality correction → S3 changes its current decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A coding review or trace report can be useful audit evidence, but without an S3 owner and explicit corrective audit mandate it is not an autonomous S3 function. - Evidence: agentflow/success.py#L1, agentflow/traces.py#L1, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L1100, agentflow/graphoptimizer.py#L75. - Basis: Negative after inspecting first-party reviewers, validation and run traces, not just class names. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An independent compliance inspector could be implemented by a downstream graph user but is not inherent in this distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Use real task-graph outcomes and traces to form a prospective changed pipeline, accept a structurally valid design and run the revised structure in subsequent optimization rounds of a specifically enabled session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Shifting coding task difficulties, graph dependency bottlenecks, recurring node failures, inefficient concurrency, misunderstood prompts and performance/trace differences across rounds. - External distinction: A completed child-run RunRecord and trace files reveal what each node attempted, output, failure, timing and status. buildgraphreport exports this actual operational evidence to the optimizer prompt. - Future / prospective distinction: The model-backed selected optimizer (Codex/Claude/Kimi/Pi) receives node outcomes/trace context after the graph run and is directed to revise pipeline.py for the next task-graph round, not just continue the failed current node. - Adaptation option generated: The model changes pipeline node prompts, dependencies, ordering, graph composition and concurrency/fail-fast settings; future alternative graph design is an authored executable Python PIPELINE rather than an inert analysis paragraph. - Path back into current capability / S3: rungraphoptimizationsession executes the selected model CLI, reloads the edited pipeline.py through loadpipelinefrompath, validates PipelineSpec, updates and persists currentpipeline, and passes it into the next createqueuedrun. This returns to next S1 graph configuration and operations, not to an independently evidenced S3 organizational executive. It is opt-in and bounded to the configured optimization session; validation is structural, not measured improvement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The selected model-backed optimizer chooses prospective graph content; first-party loader/schema and operator-defined settings gate acceptance, and the orchestration engine persists/executes accepted revision. - Decision owner: Model-backed Codex/Claude/Kimi/Pi optimizer through first-party prompt/adapter/execution path, bounded by validation and optimizer plus nrun&gt;1 host opt-in; no first-party constitutional or general cross-organization authority claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Graph reports, trace copy, editable pipeline artifact, optimizer prompt, runoptimizer adapter, retry on invalid edits, schema verifier, persisted RunStore, next child graph run and nrun bound. - Closure path: Current-round external task graph outcomes → trace/report → optimizer prospective graph edit → schema-valid newly loaded PipelineSpec → accepted updated currentpipeline stored → subsequent run of new graph → new outcome/feedback. - Boundary reachability: PipelineSpec publicly exposes optimizer and nrun, Orchestrator.submit selects rungraphoptimizationsession; it invokes runoptimizer backed by first-party CLI adapter, validates/persists revised graph, then launches a new child graph run. README documents this exact opt-in mode. nrun=1 or no optimizer does not activate this S4 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model-backed optimizer, scheduler, traces, schema gate and retry machinery cannot create purpose-sensitive new graph structure from observed execution failures. Conversely, without graph loader/runner, the model&#39;s suggestion would not be executed; the positive is the integrated shipped composed mode and not a claim of reliable measured improvement. - Evidence: README.md#L366, README.md#L375, agentflow/specs.py#L2332, agentflow/specs.py#L2430, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L354, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L428, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L478, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L499, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L539, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L569, agentflow/orchestrator.py#L609, agentflow/tunedagents.py#L390. - Basis: First-party source-linked model-option-to-future-run adoption; no provider-run or reward/quality uplift claims. - Confidence: High for structural path, medium for runtime practical success without external CLI/provider. - Caveats: Validation only establishes that new pipeline loads, not that revised actions improve result; the updated graph is scoped to that optimization session and saved run artifacts. No autonomous permanent update of all future user-created pipelines is evidenced.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate decision owner of ultimate organizational identity/purpose, with a binding returned constitutional policy, is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider credentials, exposed host workspaces and unsafe Docker/host permissions pose risks; static tool access and user-selected sandbox bound work without an identity-level dispute adjudicator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host/application config chooses providers, tools, credential injection, network policy, allowed workspace and graph goals. First-party adapter and runner enforce or translate those constraints, but do not decide enduring organizational ultimate purpose. - Decision owner: External user/operator authority, deterministic first-party runner preflights and tool configuration; no S5 governance closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolAccess read-only/read-write, Docker secret isolation and network policy, scoped CLI config, user graph validation, failure results/logging. - Closure path: Operator permissions/constraints → prepared runner command or denial/exception → task continues or stops; no ultimate-purpose question → legitimate S5 judgment → durable identity policy returned. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Command authorization and model provider safety parameters govern local execution, not the identity or policy of an independent viable organization. - Evidence: agentflow/agents/codex.py#L13, agentflow/agents/pi.py#L17, agentflow/runners/docker.py#L1, agentflow/specs.py#L2332, README.md#L132. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking provider/tool security, graph controls and optimization modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human owner of the application above this graph may exercise legitimate S5 outside AgentFlow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f200ff3cfe8e986f03c8cb03d743b49f3d2ed89b/assessments/ag/agentflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f200ff3cfe8e986f03c8cb03d743b49f3d2ed89b\">f200ff3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T01:55:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Coral-Bricks-AI/reef\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reef</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Coral-Bricks-AI/reef/tree/33aefaa12d78891bf5ba70a0d96b070d471ecc78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 33aefaa12d78891bf5ba70a0d96b070d471ecc78\">33aefaa &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Goal-facing operational task execution, covering the ReAct model/tool loop, AlphaCumen&#39;s finite finance analysis requests, and Polyp&#39;s next queued research experiment execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable user queries and retrieved data, model-generated tool calls, specialist output, failed model/tool requests, uncertain results, workload failures, GPU capacity and experiment deviations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured LLM selects next tool use and stops in Reef; AlphaCumen planner/synthesizer issues specialist tasks and chooses whether another analytical round is needed; Polyp&#39;s architect coding agent/worker executes a concrete scheduled experimental work unit. - Decision owner: Reef external configured model acting through first-party ReAct; AlphaCumen per-query synthesizer/specialist models; Polyp&#39;s model-backed Claude agent processes acting via first-party phase wrappers. Caller retains task, tool permission and scientific target. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reef.llm provider adapters, runreact tool dispatch/trajectory, AlphaCumen roster and common thread, Polyp queue claim/leases, worker heartbeat/recovery, result.json and scientific verdict transitions. - Closure path: User/queue task → model-directed action or specialist delegation → tool/worker result → returned observations and continuation/final output; experiment branches/specs produce results and are finalized into evidence and queue status. - Boundary reachability: Bundled Python source implements each path; ReAct dispatches direct provider calls; AlphaCumen invokes first-party Reef loop with specialist config; Polyp watcher and scripts call HeadlessClaude and queue commands to perform actions, not merely record prompt templates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model reasoning leaves only data transport, static constraints and queue scheduling: the next exploratory tool choice, qualitative analysis or authored code is not reproduced by the deterministic scaffolding. - Evidence: reef/react.py; reef/llm.py; alphacumen/swarm.py; alphacumen/roster.py; polyp/runner/architectcodetask.py; polyp/runner/workershift.py; polyp/queue/cbq.py; reef/agents/headlessclaude.py. - Basis: Shipped source-wiring for supported separate products, structural. - Confidence: High for real agent-loop wiring; no empirical model outcomes repeated here. - Caveats: Three separately installable subsystems are alternative first-party modes, not automatically one co-resident distributed organization. AlphaCumen depends on external configured services and Polyp depends on headless Claude and operator-supplied worker hardware.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independently decisive relation demonstrably attenuates mutual operational interference between separately viable S1 business units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AlphaCumen parallel specialist analysis may overlap or disagree; Polyp work on CPU/GPU may contend for resources; queued tasks may race. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner controls AlphaCumen specialist invocations under one question; Polyp queue leases and watchers serialize jobs, manage slots and stale claims. Neither is evidenced as separate inter-S1 organizational negotiation/feedback among viable departments. - Decision owner: Per-request synthesizer or deterministic queue/watcher; no separately mandated S2 over viable operational S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Specialist roster/fan-out/commonthread, process pool, postgres SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, locks/leases/heartbeat and worker reconciliation. - Closure path: Task outputs return to synthesizer and results to experiment queue; resource contention is bounded by slot/claim machinery but no distinct S1-to-S1 reciprocal conflict damping changes interdepartmental behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Seven domain personas operate as task-scoped specialist invocations of one finance query. Polyp workers/architects are phases of the same experiment pipeline rather than separately viable customer-facing organizational divisions. S2 cannot be conferred by fan-out or locks. - Evidence: alphacumen/swarm.py; alphacumen/roster.py; polyp/queue/cbq.py; polyp/runner/architectwatcher.py; polyp/runner/workerwatcher.py; polyp/runner/README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting both specialist and CPU/GPU queue coordinating routes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A deployment with truly independent research departments might add an S2 at a different organizational boundary, not evidenced by this distributed workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated agent-owned inside-and-now managerial portfolio/regulator over separately viable continuing S1 departments in a shared mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist quality/coverage and costs, failed experimental tasks and GPU capacity stress a current run or queued portfolio. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AlphaCumen GP selects which specialists to invoke per single query; Polyp watcher scheduler moves queued phases, and code/analysis agents revise individual experiment verdicts. Neither has independent whole-organizational current manager authority reallocating viable S1 departmental obligations. - Decision owner: Single-task model planner and first-party per-stage deterministic scheduler; human owner supplies scientific research mission/constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Max rounds, pruning, per-tool limits and asof clamps; queue SQL status machine, worker heartbeat, capacity checks and code/analysis retries. - Closure path: Current request/work item → narrower invocation, stop, retry, park or archive. No evidenced viable-department status→system-wide discretionary resource/commitment decision→binding returned adjustments to those departments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A synthesizer GP name does not establish S3. The watcher CPU/GPU slot scheduler executes predetermined claim rules; its per-task control is not an agent-owned managerial law of a network of viable operating units. - Evidence: alphacumen/swarm.py; reef/enforcement.py; reef/constraints.py; polyp/queue/cbq.py; polyp/runner/architectwatcher.py; polyp/runner/workerwatcher.py; polyp/runner/workershift.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after testing agent planner and queue scheduler as plausible S3 candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A real Coral Bricks organization/operator could decide budgets and direction externally; no first-party manager-right return is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary verification of management&#39;s picture of live operating reality feeding back corrective recommendations to a distinguishable whole-current S3 manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Finance findings might be inconsistent, experiment outcomes might be invalid, worker deviations might remain undocumented or obscure failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Polyp&#39;s Analyzer agent independently reads a finished experiment&#39;s results/deviations and posts scientific done/falsified/blocked verdict in the queue. Reef AuditingEnforcer records constraint checks and AlphaCumen traces calls. They do not directly audit managerial current-state reports with challenge/returned S3 correction. - Decision owner: Experiment analyzer model and deterministic recorders own their narrower scientific evaluation/audit trail; no designated independent management-auditor function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: polyp architectanalyzetask, queue cbq verdict status and archives, Reef AuditingEnforcer, model trajectory logging, AlphaCumen per-run traces. - Closure path: Experiment evidence→scientific acceptance/falsification→queue archive/constraint update→future hypothesis generation; not audit of whole-operational control report→distinct S3 decision→corrective operating action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent judge of experiment validity is a substantive assessment of experimental output but is not automatically a VSM S3 audit. The separate organizational current-management owner and alternate reality-information channel are missing. - Evidence: polyp/runner/architectanalyzetask.py; polyp/queue/cbq.py; reef/enforcement.py; alphacumen/swarm.py; polyp/runner/suggestexperiment.py. - Basis: Bounded absence with explicit inspection of the strongest positive candidate, the Polyp Analyzer. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Future external field-audit or independent expert inspection would be out of scope. Scientific falsification feeding future research direction is credited under that function, not as S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence for Polyp&#39;s future research-campaign direction: choose prospective experimental hypotheses and future work lines based on past campaign results and constraints, then actually queue their execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Previous experiments may plateau, falsify hypotheses or reveal blockers; idle iteration and GPU availability create a need to choose a different research trajectory rather than continue an unproductive line. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured headless Claude Auto-suggester can autonomously OPEN a new research line or CONTINUE/reopen one skipped by per-experiment iterators, based on whole prior done/falsified/blocked history, and execute cbq submit --origin auto-suggest for the next experiment. A queued accepted task drives later coding/worker/analyze cycles. - Decision owner: Polyp auto-suggester LLM proposes which future hypothesis/line warrants a program change; deterministic watcher admits when portfolio is quiet and a worker is live, Postgres queue commits the resulting candidate for later work. Human constrains research interests but is not required to select each suggested candidate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: architectwatcher generator trigger gated by live worker and low-watermark; suggestexperiment prompt reads portfolio history and constraints and calls HeadlessClaude; cbq submit validates and persists next task; subsequent architectcodetask and workerwatcher/shift execute it and analyzer returns verdict. - Closure path: Completed/falsified/blocked experiment evidence and constraints → autonomous portfolio-level future-line decision → persisted Postgres next-experiment submission with origin/parent → new code+worker experimental actions in subsequent campaign iteration → fresh experimental evidence feeding the next generator. Can select an entirely new direction, not just rerun the last task. - Boundary reachability: Polyp is separately installable and explicitly ships this generator path: architectwatcher invokes suggestexperiment only on idle/low-water queue, suggestion LLM can write a task spec and invoke cbq submit; queue status machine hands it to real first-party coding/worker watchers. No proprietary Coral Bricks service, undocumented evaluator or benchmark marketing is needed to establish the structural admission/return route, though runtime requires Postgres/Claude and worker. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the auto-suggester model while retaining queue, watchers and worker: the portfolio&#39;s discretionary choice of which not-yet-planned hypothesis/new line to try no longer occurs. The fixed heartbeat and queue do not substitute a future intelligence decision. This is S4 specifically at the bounded research-program recursion, not proof of corporate market intelligence or a universal S4 for Reef/AlphaCumen. - External distinction: The Polyp generator inspects research outcomes and constraints collected outside the presently executing experiment: portfolio-wide done/falsified/blocked history, lessons from earlier research lines, and new feasibility information. It runs when current iterators are quiet, not as a tool call of one experiment; this is the bounded experimental-program environment, not corporate market sensing. - Future / prospective distinction: The generator decides which future experimental family/hypothesis is worth pursuing when the present iteration stops or misses a question, including reopening prior lines for documented new reasons. It does not merely correct the latest answer or retry a single failed step. - Adaptation option generated: A newly authored research task specification including method, hypothesis and rationale, selecting a genuinely new or previously overlooked research line; the model can submit through the Postgres experiment queue with origin auto-suggest, or abstain if nothing is worthwhile. - Path back into current capability / S3: A submitted suggestion becomes a durable enqueued Postgres work item; the existing architect watcher claims it for code/spec construction, a worker executes it, and an analyzer records results that inform the next selection. This changes subsequent research-program operation; it does not imply a separate S3 executive or corporate policy authority. - Evidence: polyp/README.md; polyp/runner/README.md; polyp/runner/suggestexperiment.py; polyp/runner/architectwatcher.py; polyp/runner/architectcodetask.py; polyp/queue/cbq.py; polyp/runner/workerwatcher.py; polyp/runner/architectanalyzetask.py. - Basis: Explicit supported end-to-end structural return path, with model-authored portfolio-level future-option selection and recorded queue submission. - Confidence: Medium-high for Polyp shipped research-campaign S4; low for wider corporate strategy. - Caveats: Effectiveness of proposed hypotheses and actual unattended deployment remain unmeasured here. The route is triggered only when conditions are met and supports a human veto/constraints; do not upgrade to A(P) because no distinct first-party binding S4 parent-governed mode is shown. Changing the experiment program is not changing Reef framework&#39;s global mission.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate identity/constitutional-purpose policy decider whose returned decision binds subsequent operations of the assessed multi-product org or research-program mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific risk constraints, time cutoffs, permitted datasets, human vetoes and domain policies bound runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller passes HarnessConstraints, enforcer applies asof/tool limits; Polyp auto-suggester must respect explicit human-provided interest, constraints and allowed experiment kinds; it cannot change charter-level mission/values. CLI stop/cancel and the experiment rule are externally set. - Decision owner: Researchers, embedding developers and upstream human organization set mission, allowed topics, governance and permitted hardware; no shipped first-party S5 authority-return workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessConstraints, deterministic LocalEnforcer, experiment constraints.md checks, cbq cancel/stop, permission/queue origin flags and developer prompt templates. - Closure path: Human author-controlled rules → task admission/constraints; no identity-policy concern→legitimate parent deliberation/decision→new binding organizational constitutional policy returned to continuing S1/S3/S4 owners. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An experimental optimizer that selects future experiments within its provided user goal still cannot redefine the research institution&#39;s identity. Enforced scientific constraints and a veto window do not establish S5=P without authoritative constitutional decision closure. - Evidence: reef/constraints.py; reef/enforcement.py; polyp/queue/cbq.py; polyp/runner/suggestexperiment.py; polyp/runner/architectwatcher.py; polyp/README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after examining explicit constraints and operator veto in all public modes. - Confidence: High at the published code boundary. - Caveats: A private parent Coral Bricks policy process is excluded; cannot infer a first-party S5 from branding or institutional existence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a1d500291c4f4bd564fb62b3ef73fbd784516606/assessments/re/reef.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a1d500291c4f4bd564fb62b3ef73fbd784516606\">a1d5002 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:05:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/k4yt3x/meka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">meka</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/k4yt3x/meka/tree/b7c188a831f888e8accecc1bbe7bd95a7a0112c8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b7c188a831f888e8accecc1bbe7bd95a7a0112c8\">b7c188a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete a user&#39;s coding/research/general assistant task, or a delegated task in a scoped subagent, through model-selected tool actions and observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user needs, unpredictable shell/file/web/MCP observations, incomplete memory, tool errors and subtask requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each model decides whether to use shell, file, memory, research, scheduling, subagent or other admitted tools and chooses its subsequent course after results. - Decision owner: each active meka model-backed session/worker in the first-party agent turn loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, registry/tool discovery, tool dispatch and results, persistent sessions, skills, memory, checklist, permission levels and optional scheduled/background turns. - Closure path: user/scheduled/background/parent prompt → provider response with selected tool calls → dispatch gated execution → result blocks returned to the same agent → model revision or answer. - Boundary reachability: shipped CLI/ACP/HTTP use the same agent core; agentspawn builds a real narrowed worker and calls the agent turn loop, so multi-worker S1 is not a mere README topology. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model selects operational actions and revises them under observations; scheduled triggers, scope admission, memory persistence and fallback enforcement are supporting mechanics. - Evidence: turn code, dispatch, subagent spawn, README. - Basis: frozen implementation and shipped user/tool contracts. - Confidence: high structurally; no paid provider E2E executed in this review. - Caveats: the model&#39;s tool rights depend on runtime permission, optional settings and host credentials. Subagents can be disabled; that removes multi-unit positive modes without removing single-agent S1.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent edit interference between independent parent/child or sibling agent work cells using shared first-party file tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two separate agent sessions attempt to edit the same disk/editor file based on stale or concurrently changing contents, risking lost updates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: host-scoped file guard serializes one path&#39;s read/check/edit/write, refuses stale changes, and forces a later agent/tool call to re-read or explicitly override. - Decision owner: constructor-owned WriteLocks and ReadTracker/stalereadcomplaint logic; the model/caller must choose follow-up conflict resolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: canonical path keys, shared per-host async mutex, per-agent read stamp, compare against disk/editor buffer, conflict error tool result and direct-child workspace bounds. - Closure path: agent A reads/writes a file → agent B&#39;s older stamped edit arrives → same-path lock plus freshness check rejects B&#39;s stale write → error returns to B&#39;s model, which can re-read/re-plan; non-conflicting paths proceed. - Boundary reachability: SessionMaterials carries one shared host-wide WriteLocks to parent and worker registry scopes, and first-party fileedit takes scope.lockpath over the read-modify-write step; a specific source-level cross-registry collision hazard is documented and tested. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict detection/serialization is deterministic constructor ownership (C), not negotiated by an autonomous S2 model. The agent may respond, but the first-party regulator specifically targets cross-cell interference. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently active parent and spawned child or two sibling subagents, each with independent model turn and local read history. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a shared workspace file is modified after another S1&#39;s read, or simultaneous file edits would overwrite one another. - Attenuating coordination relation: host-shared canonical-path lock and version/freshness validation establish write serialization and refuse stale intent. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: returned changed on disk/editor error instructs re-read; the worker model sees the refusal and can adjust its next file operation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the first-party mechanism is explicitly shared across separate agent registries to resolve an actual write/write race and provide corrective feedback, not merely to queue one conversation&#39;s calls. - Evidence: WriteLocks rationale, file edit lock/freshness, background concurrency guide. - Basis: first-party source mechanism plus source tests for stale-read rejection, bounded positive. - Confidence: medium-high for file paths, not for arbitrary shell/MCP operations. - Caveats: the guide correctly warns no universal coordination: shell writes and external tools can bypass these guards, and force can override freshness. This is a bounded constructor path, not end-to-end team settlement.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current direct-child task portfolio when an agent has actually spawned subagents and is responsible for the joint user goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving subtasks, incomplete/late child reports, need to redirect a running worker, redundant continuation and parent/worker permission changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model decides which independent tasks to delegate, what rights/scope each gets, whether to follow up or steer/interrupt a running child, and when to discard an obsolete worker. - Decision owner: parent meka model with the shipped agentspawn, agentlist, agentfollowup, agentsteer, agentdelete tools; Rust enforces membership and scope ceilings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted session parent-child rows, child reports, agentlist direct-child roster and activity, in-flight interrupt/steer inbox, conversation lookup, parent checklist and bounded recursion. - Closure path: parent sees its task commitments and child reports/roster → model selects new assignment/follow-up/steer/interrupt/delete → first-party child session receives revised work or termination → next child report returns to parent. - Boundary reachability: multi-subagent mode is built into the default tool registry (when depth permits); agentlist loads actual direct-child session rows, agentsteer writes the child&#39;s inbox, agentfollowup resumes persisted child under recorded grants. No external orchestrator plugin is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decision to change child commitments belongs to the model parent; direct-child membership, permission and inbox delivery are deterministic enforcement. The single-agent mode does not by itself supply this S3. - Whole-system current view: parent session&#39;s current task/checklist plus directly spawned child identifiers, directories, turn/activity records, reports and accessible child conversations at this explicitly bounded recursion. - Current-control decision scope: initiate/reallocate subtask effort among children, change a running child&#39;s instructions, interrupt superseded work, resume or delete child resources under the owner&#39;s chosen parent goal. - Evidence: subagent lifecycle and model tools, subagents guide, checklist. - Basis: frozen implemented optional multi-worker mode with explicit current-control rights. - Confidence: medium; closure is source-reachable, no actual multi-agent model-run transcript inspected. - Caveats: direct-child visibility is bounded, not a global entire-installation fleet control; ordinary one-off delegation without reassessment/steer would remain task-level routing and should not be used alone as S3 evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent authority checks live agent completion claims against a separate evidentiary path and returns a veto to S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child may submit inaccurate final reports or a parent may declare an unchecked task complete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: checklist nudges stop an agent with its own open commitments, while parent can read child conversations; neither is an automatically independent outcome audit. - Decision owner: same parent/child model and deterministic checklist; no separately commissioned auditor with distinct reality access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: list states, bounded completion nudges, status/turn logs, child reports and persistent inbox. - Closure path: checklist can force same agent to continue/cancel/defer; parent can manually inspect another session&#39;s transcript, but no shipped complementary inspector/adjudicator returns a finding to whole-system control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: same-agent task assertions and log replay do not independently verify raw artifacts or challenge their meaning. - Evidence: checklist guide, subagent tools, README. - Basis: frozen source and scoped negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a parent may deliberately spawn an independent test agent with raw artifact access, but generic worker creation plus task wording alone does not establish a supplied independent audit mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future/environment sensing and resulting capability adaptation decision loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing web/MCP data, future time triggers, new tools and user-created skills; each can be observed on demand or scheduled. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can schedule future ordinary tasks, call web tools, write configured skills or switch provider profiles; no shipped prospective option assessment owns adaptation and returns a capability change. - Decision owner: task agent/operator acting through general tools, not a distinct future-facing intelligence function at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: schedulecreate with a predicate gate, webfetch, tool discovery, optional agent-managed skill and profile selection. - Closure path: scheduled observation → new regular S1 turn; skill write may change an operator-configured instruction package, but no first-party prospective environmental model/options/consequence/decision return is specified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-scheduling or researching today&#39;s environment extends S1 operations without a closed external-and-future S4 adaptive right. - Evidence: scheduling guide, skills guide, registry. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external agents can build such a workflow using meka primitives, but this assessment does not credit hypothetical composition.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-policy/identity authority with a returned governance decision for this local agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user permission levels, tool approvals, model/skill choices and persisted capability restrictions, not contests over institutional identity or ultimate purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/operator grants read/workspace/unrestricted authority or approves individual tool calls; subagent grants may only narrow and never widen past parent. - Decision owner: human operator for runtime permissions and parent-supplied goal, not a first-party S5 identity/policy deliberator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission ladder, approval callbacks, follow-up scope clamp, ToolScope filters and sandboxing. - Closure path: a tool call may be permitted/refused, and a later parent permission change limits children; no policy-identity issue is escalated for legitimate collective authority and returned as a standing new organizational purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action-level consent and fixed safety limits remain bounded runtime authorization, not the S5 viability policy function. - Evidence: permission guide, subagent grant replay, dispatch approval. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a parent institution could use meka as a tool while retaining S5 outside this software; such external governance is not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d1835adb542cc687ce45bee363dcd13f75cf3c1/assessments/me/meka.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d1835adb542cc687ce45bee363dcd13f75cf3c1\">2d1835a &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
